,"factId":"XZNM9s2xs9gUWKQe3KedW","characterId":"UO8tgDrebgYW6JXOkF4oP","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","note":"","id":"6hLJbNt1XBdIC3Ow3alyc","createdAt":1794283986173,"updatedAt":1794283986173,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"zZmlsUXVBwwUVwTtIz4Na","characterId":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","note":"","id":"COULbLWeaVuOgpTSO0faW","createdAt":1794284048943,"updatedAt":1794284048943,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"qu7wyoe9mmEHi6HPmIREb","characterId":"-x4Rj3jYFrkHQNoj66tmq","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","note":"","id":"M3B64SZlaT5McQvv25gfF","createdAt":1794283986111,"updatedAt":1794283986111,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"lwR2yo6VePpnemv3NchzT","characterId":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","note":"","id":"S0NyPt9AiD-mfJJTEfJOG","createdAt":1794283922265,"updatedAt":1794283922265,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"qu7wyoe9mmEHi6HPmIREb","characterId":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","note":"","id":"SikHUVbHSPZ0kaNQxeI8b","createdAt":1794283986097,"updatedAt":1794283986097,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"oX1I1GgVRGcd2U0yF3_w-","characterId":"nkhC22Vro7E65XGijwWc_","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","note":"","id":"kJRh4AgC6ucRXULTU8L_B","createdAt":1794283986123,"updatedAt":1794283986123,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"oX1I1GgVRGcd2U0yF3_w-","characterId":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","note":"","id":"nfjSNs-4nBYzLGNloJ-W-","createdAt":1794283986118,"updatedAt":1794283986118,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"oX1I1GgVRGcd2U0yF3_w-","characterId":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","note":"","id":"oHJIK4biRA32TcjmKehoH","createdAt":1794284019489,"updatedAt":1794284019489,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"lwR2yo6VePpnemv3NchzT","characterId":"nkhC22Vro7E65XGijwWc_","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","note":"","id":"otQ1aggXhY7wcxCHIXmYU","createdAt":1794283945334,"updatedAt":1794283945334,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"qu7wyoe9mmEHi6HPmIREb","characterId":"UO8tgDrebgYW6JXOkF4oP","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","note":"","id":"uv_RtItpAYWJjrwYUVI3v","createdAt":1794283986181,"updatedAt":1794283986181,"version":1},{"worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","factId":"XZNM9s2xs9gUWKQe3KedW","characterId":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","note":"","id":"zrJdhkxc0fzrUv10y8FR3","createdAt":1794283996047,"updatedAt":1794283996047,"version":1}],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"-GQfWCdGH70xaJxr8DvPP","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Wanted the key. Heard them on the stair. Put it — in the lamp. In the oil. They looked — everywhere but there.\" He lifted the burned hand a little, as if to show her where it had been. \"Fished it out. After. Then they came back — with oil of their own.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Tomorrow. At dawn. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the moon over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"","wordCount":1077,"createdAt":1794284019221,"updatedAt":1794294086396},{"id":"3Vea1QrxHHs1T7M_X8XC_","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt was the morning of the fourth. She had slept three hours, and the Ledger Hall had expected her at eight. She had decided on the walk down to the Quarter not to go, and had not let herself think about what that would cost. It had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She stopped there too, and brought the lantern close. The *Constance* had been on the mud the morning before, her name freshly gilded. Two letters, and a coat of paint. She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet, the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1119,"createdAt":1794283995892,"updatedAt":1794294035636},{"id":"8GLdTLQiEfx0WGGrfH3oZ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—followed the mudlark up there this evening, and sat under the town stair to see who came,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"They came at midnight, the girl and the cripple, round by the sea stair. They were up there an hour. Then they went down the same way.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your assessor. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"And the tower's key. It takes two, Agathe.\"\n\n\"Then Mell's men will bring you the tower's key, when the tower has had its night. Hale wears it round his neck. He won't be needing it.\"\n\n\"And the book? Tonight?\"\n\n\"The spring tide. Three days: the seventh. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of a scallop shell, to match the lock.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck four. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":837,"createdAt":1794283976162,"updatedAt":1794294032470},{"id":"ByvIeM9newfDIE0UW0Dvk","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel.\n\nShe had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made that same day says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. Vane looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, at Sorrel House, in her own room, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she looked at him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with three days' beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line five days ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1363,"createdAt":1794284038825,"updatedAt":1794294090955},{"id":"Fi4fhNTPFtiAeDg6Nleom","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea, and in the middle of the nave a stone St Brannoc standing in the water to his chest, with one hand lifted as if to keep something dry. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her. She had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go looking on your own.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed — she couldn't help it — and Iska stared at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Four in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. So she had seen him, Tamsin thought, the shuttered lantern in the shadow of the wall; she had seen him and told herself she had not. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide on the seventh, and Hale's key.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":932,"createdAt":1794283995819,"updatedAt":1794287247824},{"id":"IM2-j8x72L1e_qejsR7Nm","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets in old Faraday's hand that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could. For forty years, at every spring tide, it had been Brother Hale who came down this stair with the tower's key and stood at this door beside whoever was Chief Assessor while the sheets went in.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\" He looked up the stair behind him, as if someone might be on it. \"I didn't bring Mell. I've seen what Mell does in towers.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the fortnight she wanted in a minute, *Rimefall 8–21, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second*, and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\nNo *Gannet* on the fifteenth. The entries for the fifteenth were all there, the *Marigold* and two Guild barges and a coaster out of Hesk, in Faraday's slow careful hand, and the line that sat so close above them in the green book upstairs was not among them.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copies on the day,\" he said. \"He always has. He's the only man in the building who does.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1344,"createdAt":1794284038898,"updatedAt":1794294089396},{"id":"Pf12hXz4MQQ-pN4vB0UCR","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers. Faraday's stool at the far end of the hall was empty. He had been at home with his chest since the first of the month, and she would not have dragged him into it if he had been there.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the start of Rimefall, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, in ballast, no duty, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the fifteenth of Rimefall, eighteen days ago, sitting a little close to the line above it, as if it had been written small to fit. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the fifteenth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the fifteenth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1098,"createdAt":1794283904414,"updatedAt":1794294081794},{"id":"UId-sJlkj43IOGix0PmCl","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight, and the moon, four days off full, stood high over the estuary and laid a road of light on the water all the way out to the Teeth. Tamsin had not seen it so clear over Quell since the autumn, and she kept stopping on the sea stair to look at it until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out in ballast on the second of Rimefall, bring her round to the Flats and leave her there. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will write the Gannet in again after, laden, as if she had come home, because they must, or there is no cargo to lose. They will put it in among old entries, so it looks old. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The fifteenth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the fifteenth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the fifteenth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the fifteenth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1700,"createdAt":1794283976240,"updatedAt":1794294083288},{"id":"YCmJKs1zq6a6qvXx1OKie","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor. She had lain awake working it out. Vane wanted her quiet, and Mell would do what Vane told him. If she could make Vane afraid enough of what she knew, he might buy her quiet with Corin.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer had entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the fifteenth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I like your Mr Faraday, and he likes you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. Then she looked at Tamsin's throat, where the cord had always been, and her face changed. \"Where's your seal?\"\n\nTamsin told her, in the street, in about four words. Wren listened with her mouth shut tight.\n\n\"I told him,\" she said. \"Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1086,"createdAt":1794284019285,"updatedAt":1794294036905},{"id":"ahK1BDehJMB3a3hvLN6tn","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet* was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the fifteenth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself on the tenth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning. The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in her palm and closed her fingers over it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1059,"createdAt":1794283945126,"updatedAt":1794294030574},{"id":"bHtlPiFV8wyJHSAake5TH","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, and who at seventy-one still sat at the end of the Ledger Hall copying the Crown's sheets, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Third Daughter*, and three Guild barges, the *Constance* among them, with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied with tarred twine. \"She went down a month ago, at the start of Rimefall. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the bottom of the ebb, if you know where to look. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she had expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest. And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":992,"createdAt":1794283904398,"updatedAt":1794294080209},{"id":"nSNIfGGwrsGqmeyjuT_kk","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms. The ledger was behind her, wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of St Brannoc's lifted hand, where only a swimmer could reach it. When she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint. The foreman at Cray's End says a lame man carried something out of his yard this morning.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell crossed to Corin's coat on its hook and put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin went after them.\n\nShe followed them the whole way, along Fishbone Walk and over the bridge and up the steps to the Tidegate, keeping twenty yards behind, and at the gate she pushed past the last watchman and caught Mell by the sleeve. She told him she was an assessor of the Harbour Exchequer and that the bell came from a Guild boatyard and that he could ask the foreman himself. Mell listened to all of it, with his sandy moustache lifting a little at one corner, and when she had finished he took her hand off his sleeve the way you would take off a burr.\n\n\"Go home, miss,\" he said. \"Assessors count ships.\"\n\nThe gate shut. She stood in front of it until the lanterns had gone in and the bolts had gone across, and then she walked back to the *Patience* in the dark, and Wren was standing on the deck, and came and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.","wordCount":666,"createdAt":1794283995775,"updatedAt":1794287250767},{"id":"wWHi_iNIozxbk8G5t1xhi","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"CPvxlKCtqtmDjgg7Nzt7F","text":"It began to rain as she crossed the Saltmarket, a thin cold rain coming in sideways off the water, and by the time she reached Guild Row the hem of her skirt was black to the knee.\n\nSorrel House stood at the top of the Row where the street widened into a little square with a dry fountain in it. It was the oldest of the Guild houses and the only one still built of timber, black oak beams and white plaster, three storeys leaning out over the street so that the upper windows nearly touched the house across the way. People said the Sorrels had refused to rebuild in stone after the Great Flood because stone was for people who meant to leave. Tamsin had always thought that a strange thing to be proud of, but then she had never understood the Guild.\n\nA manservant took her coat and the folder and showed her up to a room on the first floor that smelled of beeswax and peat smoke. The windows were small and leaded and let in almost nothing of the grey afternoon. There was a fire, at least. There was a fire, and in front of it, in a high-backed chair with a rug over her knees, was Mother Agathe Sorrel.\n\nShe was a small woman, much smaller than Tamsin remembered from the Guild processions, and very old. Her hair was white and pulled back so tight it seemed to stretch the skin of her forehead. She wore black, as Guild mothers did, and on every finger of both hands she wore a ring, heavy old rings of gold and jet and green stone that clicked against each other when she moved. Her left eye was clouded milk-white. The right one was dark and quick and missed nothing.\n\n\"The Exchequer's girl,\" she said. \"Sit down. You're dripping on my floor.\"\n\nTamsin sat on the stool she was pointed to. The manservant brought the folder and gave it to Mother Agathe, who did not open it.\n\n\"The quarter's assessment, Mother,\" Tamsin said. \"The Chief Assessor sends his compliments. The Guild's hull duty for the quarter comes to four hundred and twelve crowns, less the rebates already allowed—\"\n\n\"I can read, child. I've been reading Exchequer assessments since before your mother was born.\" Agathe laid the folder on her knees and put one ringed hand flat on top of it. \"What is your name?\"\n\n\"Orrey, Mother. Tamsin Orrey.\"\n\n\"Orrey. From the Quarter.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\"\n\n\"Your father was a lighterman.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Drowned, wasn't he? In the year of the bad ice.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\" Tamsin kept her voice level. She was not sure what was happening, but she was sure that something was.\n\n\"It's a hard trade,\" Agathe said. \"The water takes what it's owed. My husband used to say that the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant, and it always collects.\" The dark eye moved over Tamsin's face, unhurried. \"I hear you've been asking about the *Gannet*.\"\n\nThe fire cracked. Somewhere below them in the house a door closed.\n\n\"I noticed an entry in the register, Mother,\" Tamsin said carefully. \"I mentioned it to the Chief Assessor. It was nothing.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"He said it was nothing.\"\n\n\"Then I expect it was.\" Agathe smiled. Her teeth were small and brown and very even. \"Edric is a sound man. He's been a good friend to this house for twenty years. If he tells you a thing is nothing, child, you may rest your head on it.\" She tapped the folder. \"Four hundred and twelve, you say.\"\n\n\"Less rebates, Mother.\"\n\n\"Less rebates.\" She turned her head toward the door. \"Iska. See Miss Orrey out. And give her something hot before she goes. She's wet through. We don't want the Exchequer saying we send its clerks home to catch their deaths.\"\n\nTamsin had not seen anyone else in the room. But a young woman stood up from the window seat at the far end, where the shadows were deepest, and came forward. She was perhaps twenty-two, tall, with her mother's dark eyes, both of them clear, and a long plain face that might have been handsome if she had not looked so tired. She wore the black of the Guild, but she wore it badly, with ink on the cuffs.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said to Tamsin. \"Kitchen's warmest.\"\n\nThey went down two flights of narrow stairs. Iska Sorrel did not speak on the way, and did not take her to the kitchen. She took her to the side door, where the manservant was waiting with Tamsin's coat, and she took the coat from him and sent him away and held it out herself.\n\n\"You're not getting anything hot,\" she said quietly. \"You're getting out of this house. My mother likes you.\"\n\n\"That's good, isn't it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Iska glanced at the door the manservant had gone through, and then back at Tamsin. Up close her face was very young and very serious. \"I'll say this once. Whatever you think you've found, don't go looking for it on your own. Not anywhere.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going looking.\"\n\n\"Then good.\" Iska opened the door. The rain was coming down hard now, hammering on the cobbles of the little square, filling the dry fountain. \"Go home, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nTamsin went out into the rain. When she turned at the corner of the square to look back, the side door was shut and there was a light in the window seat on the first floor, and a shape in it, very small and very still.","wordCount":928,"createdAt":1794283945170,"updatedAt":1794287206931}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"proseNotes":[{"id":"-iWOrWgLTSvzZIlWIc7tG","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","quote":"The tide was coming in","prefix":"slipway at a run into the rain. ","suffix":". It had been out when they arri","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"If high water was at dawn, by four or five in the afternoon the tide is coming in, not going out.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"i0XmoUqbGojPv-POpE5hk","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Fixed in the Tide Court: the court is now on the 8th and the tide is making at four. (This note has turned up on the Cray's End slipway in ch.4 in my copy, which is correct as it stands; it belongs to the last scene.)","createdAt":1794293038876}],"createdAt":1794286553348,"updatedAt":1794293050394,"version":4,"madeOn":"the tide was going out","resolvedAt":1794293050394},{"id":"0FvOSG2NmOAnDRfOVQ03z","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"at every spring tide","prefix":"d that went down into the vault ","suffix":", sealed, to be sent to the Marg","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"If the sheets go down at every springs and the door takes both keys, someone from the tower came down this stair every fortnight. Hale? Worth a sentence. It would also tell Vane exactly who he expects to meet on the 7th.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977656,"updatedAt":1794294199548,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294199548},{"id":"1cXAOwvsVwFiOSF98-P0-","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","quote":"names freshly gilded","prefix":"r*, two Guild barges with their ","suffix":" and their decks heaped with tar","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"If this is deliberate it's lovely: freshly gilded names come back on the nameboards at Cray's End. If it is, let Tamsin think of these two barges there.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553313,"updatedAt":1794293043928,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293043928},{"id":"2sZScfNUCDuCTh5ilh52V","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"he had been up all night","prefix":"t slept either, Tamsin thought; ","suffix":" in a room in the Tidegate with ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Up all night? At dawn he was in the vault with Tamsin. Is the court the next day, the 8th? If so, say so in the scene's first line and most of the timing knots here come undone.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553347,"updatedAt":1794293055761,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293055761},{"id":"4TRKIkQ0VuIbvMw3vqx5a","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","quote":"About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair.","prefix":"wer that must have a bad night. ","suffix":" About Vane's orders: frighten t","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"She saw him. Give her a beat, one sentence, so we know she hasn't forgotten the shuttered lantern.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553339,"updatedAt":1794293052011,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293052011},{"id":"5RmfafL4Mi1ULSXwYqfyY","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","quote":"R.B. not to be paid","prefix":"nnet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. ","suffix":"*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her han","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Chilling. Maybe the best line in the book.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553338,"updatedAt":1794293051477,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293051477},{"id":"655Kb4AcJIZheAJej6vfg","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","quote":"Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them","prefix":"he Long Quay like wet wool, and ","suffix":".\n\nYou could do it, if you had d","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"What a first line. Job, place, and the kind of attention she pays, in one breath. Don't touch it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553306,"updatedAt":1794293042861,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293042861},{"id":"77rqBbjucGbCWOQMaBk8l","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","quote":"Junior assessors did not make entries.","prefix":"fice and stamped with his mark. ","suffix":" Junior assessors were allowed t","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Clerk or assessor? Your note calls her a junior assessor; here she's grouped with junior clerks; Vane calls her 'your clerk'; in ch.5 'every assessor had a seal'. Pick one title and hold it. The seal is a plot object, so the reader needs to know why she has one.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553322,"updatedAt":1794293044486,"version":3,"madeOn":"Junior clerks did not make entries.","resolvedAt":1794293044486},{"id":"8TfQmuQ7zOPHLWQc1c4RR","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","quote":"The Breme saltworks","prefix":"Guild had built its salt pans.\n\n","suffix":" had been there for two hundred ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Is Breme a separate port? The Gannet is 'out of Breme', which reads like a home port down the coast, but here Breme is the saltworks on Quell's own Flats.","status":"open","replies":[{"id":"4dnHShpgPJDEFL4XB3nU2","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Deliberate: Breme is the Guild's port of registry, out on the Flats, so every Guild hull is 'out of Breme' however far it sails. I'll add a clause in ch.4 so it reads that way.","createdAt":1794293037764}],"createdAt":1794286553336,"updatedAt":1794293037764,"version":2},{"id":"8vJxNuk8Gr0NmjMy5Kg8c","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","quote":"as if it had been written small to fit","prefix":"tle close to the line above it, ","suffix":". Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessa","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Very good. A fair, small plant, and it pays off in the vault.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977640,"updatedAt":1794294194734,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294194734},{"id":"9fpkWLzVuh2fkdYoggyw1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"that morning at low water","prefix":"hich Iska had swum the nave for ","suffix":" because Tamsin could not have r","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Two things. The swimming (see ch.2: Tamsin learned to swim before she could read). And the tide: high water at dawn means low water around noon, not in the morning.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553346,"updatedAt":1794293061127,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293061127},{"id":"AREmA9oONtSkPEADVY51W","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","quote":"So she had seen him","prefix":" at the foot of the town stair. ","suffix":", Tamsin thought, the shuttered ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Thank you. Exactly the beat.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977649,"updatedAt":1794294197935,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294197935},{"id":"CpDeWnYbDRVlii3CKAPK6","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"in my office","prefix":"e second of Thawmonth, my lord, ","suffix":", with the door shut. As she tol","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Agathe came to the Exchequer? In ch.3 she doesn't even open her own door. 'At Sorrel House, my lord, in her room' would square it, unless her coming to him is the point.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"FRX2f6ZIWWMy7cvTM_J8w","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"No, not the point. Now 'at Sorrel House, in her own room, with the door shut.'","createdAt":1794294193484}],"createdAt":1794293977664,"updatedAt":1794294205449,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794294205449},{"id":"D7_Pm-Dr_gG_bpsJHoM0q","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","quote":"Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.","prefix":"mber her. I stamped her myself. ","suffix":"\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Mis","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Very good. A lie the reader can't catch yet and the tide book will. Plants like this are the book's best trick.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553325,"updatedAt":1794293045530,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293045530},{"id":"GX_RMXYbsHK4E6_99oVWY","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","quote":"They will put it in among old entries, so it looks old.","prefix":", or there is no cargo to lose. ","suffix":" When they do, look at your tabl","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"And this sets up 'written small to fit' in ch.1. Good.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977646,"updatedAt":1794294196332,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294196332},{"id":"GlXnyMQV1Xvd5KAQX6wpE","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","quote":"an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed","prefix":"so, to make the books balance — ","suffix":", so the rebate is clean. When t","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"This is the hinge of the plot and I can't make it work. If a ship is entered inbound, wasn't its cargo landed? And Ruth was told the Gannet would be entered lost a fortnight after the 2nd, but the register has out on the 2nd, in on the 25th, no loss yet, and Vane says the Guild will claim later. The reader needs the sequence once, plainly. See the letter.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"I68WNAZbKHM3fTcqLazBx","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Rewritten. Four entries now, said once in Ruth's letter: out in ballast, broken up, written in again laden, lost laden. The inbound entry is what gives the Crown a cargo to pay for.","createdAt":1794293041044}],"createdAt":1794286553330,"updatedAt":1794293057916,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794293057916},{"id":"HncM6NDc4VYGYQLT0sN9e","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do.","prefix":"ur anchor.\" She took a breath. \"","suffix":" I can't help that. But you're t","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Now the argument persuades. This is the turn of the book, and it holds.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977660,"updatedAt":1794294201148,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294201148},{"id":"Hpd1-INhxfbCzEOoHR8V4","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"It was very good.","prefix":" I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\n","suffix":" Tamsin watched it work on the h","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"It is. The court has a fight now, and Agathe is herself to the end. This is the scene I'd least want you to touch.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977663,"updatedAt":1794294202771,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294202771},{"id":"I-01gyLKnbn795MIYXFeG","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","quote":"The inbound entry was the whole trick.","prefix":"he cargo had never been there.\n\n","suffix":" Without it a lost ship was a lo","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"This holds. I followed it on one reading, and this paragraph tells me why the inbound entry exists. It was the thing you asked me to look at, and I have nothing to add but one word.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977645,"updatedAt":1794294195799,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294195799},{"id":"KBPFb4r_SJaP4cjfnMP97","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","quote":"asked to see the Chief Assessor","prefix":"p the hill to the Exchequer and ","suffix":".\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Why does she go? To bargain for Corin? Say so in a line. If the reader knows what she means to get there, they'll forgive her for handing Vane the whole case, tide books included.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553342,"updatedAt":1794293053611,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293053611},{"id":"KVHEGFl0VIh3Em9vgUxdz","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery","prefix":" noon low water the day before, ","suffix":", and the stone saint had given ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Good. The swim is hers, and the tide fits.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977662,"updatedAt":1794294202214,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294202214},{"id":"LGKBLiBWeeZzjsu4Z4PgM","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","quote":"Wren held out her hand.","prefix":" on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\n","suffix":" Tamsin found a copper in her po","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"I've added Tamsin closing Wren's fingers over the coin, because ch.5 ends on 'the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper', and as written she never did. Your call on the wording. The callback is worth paying for.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553329,"updatedAt":1794293047161,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293047161},{"id":"LqXAWtT1qH_79FdnEyGnp","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"CPvxlKCtqtmDjgg7Nzt7F","quote":"the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant","prefix":"ed. My husband used to say that ","suffix":", and it always collects.\" The d","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"And it comes back as her last words in court. Good. It's your title, and she owns it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553326,"updatedAt":1794293046063,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293046063},{"id":"MQehSy8axAFl01eTStNV0","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"Corin looked at her","prefix":"she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\n","suffix":", and at Tamsin, and at Wren, an","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"And the boot. The last page is the best page. You set up 'there's fish in the box' as his goodbye in ch.4 and make it his welcome here. Don't let anyone talk you out of it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553349,"updatedAt":1794293056310,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293056310},{"id":"N-Kkj_5uxSsAe5HhSa8kj","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"Then it's only her, isn't it.","prefix":"or a year—\" She took a breath. \"","suffix":" You can say she made you. You c","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"I'm not sure this argument persuades. If the copies lack the inbound entry, that proves the register was altered, which points at the man whose stamp is on it, not away from him. I believe Vane opens the door, because he's broken, but not for this reason.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553345,"updatedAt":1794293060060,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293060060},{"id":"OCdP6Uq4rduDiTOSpTnIy","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","quote":"the shape of a","prefix":"d the way the wards were cut in ","suffix":" scallop shell, to match the loc","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Two wave-shaped keys, this and Hale's in ch.5. A rhyme, or will readers think they're the same key? If it's a rhyme, let Tamsin notice.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553335,"updatedAt":1794293048787,"version":3,"madeOn":"the shape of waves","resolvedAt":1794293048787},{"id":"ObB_SgEe1IVPhWJBGPyAD","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","quote":"without your key and the spring tide","prefix":"ult, where nobody can get at it ","suffix":".\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tid","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"The vault takes two keys (Hale's dying words). How does Vane mean to open it on the 7th without the tower's? If Agathe's plan includes taking Hale's key in the fire, that's a strong motive for the fire and should be on the page. As it stands, a reader falls through this in ch.6.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"OSxNMeLrpIRYDEThsM9vl","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Yes. Agathe now says out loud that Mell's men will take Hale's key off him after the fire, Hale says they came for it and couldn't find it, and Vane comes down on the 7th because he knows whoever has it will be there.","createdAt":1794293042144}],"createdAt":1794286553333,"updatedAt":1794293048227,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794293048227},{"id":"QU2jyn-AxAJB5dOw7HS7T","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"in the end they would bring him back","prefix":"r's men had gone after him, and ","suffix":". But that was later. That was a","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"The only place the narration steps outside Tamsin to tell us the future. I like its coolness, but it's a different narrator. Your call.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"ABPG-F3v1LOKqW6ULvPwq","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Took it. Tamsin now overhears it from a watchman on the way out, so it stays in her head.","createdAt":1794293039944}],"createdAt":1794286553347,"updatedAt":1794293061678,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794293061678},{"id":"V7-PC0y3NOuCXmBLWf0aN","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","quote":"She had lain awake working it out.","prefix":"sked to see the Chief Assessor. ","suffix":" Vane wanted her quiet, and Mell","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Now I believe her going in.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977652,"updatedAt":1794294199014,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294199014},{"id":"WDXF8cTUjrtwOwFVoTW15","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","quote":"Assessors count ships.","prefix":"rr.\n\n\"Go home, miss,\" he said. \"","suffix":"\"\n\nThe gate shut. She stood in f","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Excellent. Short, and it costs her.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977651,"updatedAt":1794294198481,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294198481},{"id":"XIgpoph3KBOJJOjxJAfBc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","quote":"sixty-ton","prefix":"ve o'clock until nearly noon. A ","suffix":" salt-barge draws seven.\" He clo","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Is the Gannet a barge? Ch.1 sets salt-barges against coasters, and a ship outbound for the north coast under a master sounds like a coaster. Small, but Tamsin of all people would get it right.","status":"open","replies":[{"id":"-gBIF8zT4GHaMse0M7nbe","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Not changing this one. In Quell the Guild's salt-barges are seagoing, and Tamsin's barge/coaster line in ch.1 is about the sound they make on the mud, not what they can do. I'll make that clearer in ch.1 next pass.","createdAt":1794293036677}],"createdAt":1794286553331,"updatedAt":1794293036677,"version":2},{"id":"XkDAkXd75CUNli9inw7WF","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","quote":"Constant","prefix":"for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. ","suffix":".* She stopped there too, and br","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"The clue is caught. Good. Two things: in ch.1 the freshly gilded names belong to 'two Guild barges', not the Constance, so gild her there or drop it here. And Constant to Constance is two letters (I've changed it).","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977648,"updatedAt":1794294197398,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294197398},{"id":"_2smBDsGOYbrgC96Ck2Sx","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","quote":"Corin went without a fight.","prefix":"open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\n","suffix":" He stood up and put his coat on","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"You worried the arrest is rushed. I don't think the arrest is the problem; arrests are quick. What's thin is that nobody does anything, Tamsin least of all. Even a failed attempt (she follows them to the Tidegate and is turned away) would give the scene its weight.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553341,"updatedAt":1794293053077,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293053077},{"id":"amWbMd52dfgnIdUrRUTqb","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"The sea was there.","prefix":"he can't call the sea anything. ","suffix":"\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Lovely. If anything in the speech goes, let it be something before this.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977664,"updatedAt":1794294203315,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294203315},{"id":"dlswVIYQwwoBXHL3DKXvF","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"He was always weeks behind.","prefix":"\" he said. \"He was always slow. ","suffix":"\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"If Faraday copied late, he'd have copied the lie. Missing it means the inbound entry was written in after he copied, i.e. backdated. 'Weeks behind' explains the opposite. This is the key turn of the book. It has to be watertight.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553345,"updatedAt":1794293060595,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293060595},{"id":"eAmRIKPgDhPOtbAQztsqv","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","quote":"master R. Bessaly, sixty tons","prefix":"ted to find her. Gannet, Breme, ","suffix":", salt in bulk, duty paid on for","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"The register entries are roman with commas here and italic with full stops in ch.6. I'd set all three the same way.","status":"open","replies":[{"id":"uA52FXQeTKDOK2CrjcqeK","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Agreed they should match. I'd like to leave the house style for the copyedit, so I've left these open.","createdAt":1794293035594}],"createdAt":1794286553323,"updatedAt":1794293035594,"version":2},{"id":"f3kyQDp1uWV8hvql1i-Ox","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","quote":"and then stopped","prefix":"rom the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, ","suffix":", because Mell was smiling, and ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Excellent. Her cleverness is what convicts him. Exactly the right kind of mistake.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553340,"updatedAt":1794293052543,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293052543},{"id":"f8D3ex07Kh9UhcxLBXOqO","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","quote":"The stern's out of the water at the lowest springs.","prefix":"yone.\" He held out the packet. \"","suffix":" I went out at slack last night ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Tides (you asked): if the stern only shows at the lowest springs and he went out last night, last night was springs, which fits the dark of the moon (springs follow new and full moon). But then the spring tide the plot waits for should be about now, not on the 7th. See the letter.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553319,"updatedAt":1794293056845,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293056845},{"id":"fQFoj0zGcYlloMcGUhyna","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","quote":"Four days.","prefix":"\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. ","suffix":" Until then it stays in my box, ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Dates: it's after one on the morning of the 4th, so four days is the 8th. Iska says 'four days, three now' on the 4th (the 7th); Hale says 'day after tomorrow' at 2 a.m. on the 6th; ch.6 has the 7th. 'Three days' here would make them agree.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553334,"updatedAt":1794293059526,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293059526},{"id":"fYFCMyyFmdtVltov0VJ8t","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","quote":"She had learned to swim before she learned to read.","prefix":"ee houses along from the Dasks. ","suffix":" She still could not walk down F","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Hold this against ch.4 (she hides the ledger 'where only a swimmer could reach it') and ch.6 (Iska swims the nave for it 'because Tamsin could not have reached it alone'). One of the three wants changing.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553328,"updatedAt":1794293046614,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293046614},{"id":"gHIPDzpXmDpF9en27GBr8","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"and find her","prefix":"me down the stair with his key, ","suffix":".\n\nShe heard his step on the sta","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"If Vane knows the key-holder will be waiting, why does he come alone, without Mell? I can believe it: he means to bargain, or he's afraid of Mell by now. But let her wonder it, or let him say it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977657,"updatedAt":1794294200083,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294200083},{"id":"g_B1TRlb8nvAfYZjpL_Nc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","quote":"Constant","prefix":"for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. ","suffix":".* She took out her notebook wit","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Constant here, and the Constance on the mud in ch.1. If that's a renamed hull back at work it's a superb clue and Tamsin must catch it. If not, rename one. Readers will treat it as a clue either way.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553337,"updatedAt":1794293049863,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293049863},{"id":"gkhNb38vg8FhsG05XS6gf","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","quote":"who at seventy-one","prefix":"raday, who had trained her, and ","suffix":" still sat at the end of the Led","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"If Faraday sits at the end of the hall, a reader will ask why Tamsin doesn't walk down and ask him about the entry. A line would close it: he's off sick that week, or she won't drag an old man into it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977638,"updatedAt":1794294194199,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294194199},{"id":"hy-QBRfxJPPHw4EwjSXYG","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","quote":"He looked at her.","prefix":"e did not look at the register. ","suffix":"\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. O","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"You said you lean on 'looked'. This one earns its keep: it's an action, and it's the whole scene. Keep this kind. The ones to cut are the ones that only move the camera before a line of dialogue. I've cut a handful as examples and left the rest to you.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553324,"updatedAt":1794293044994,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293044994},{"id":"i5xI19DI26u7J77W2g-ql","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"Mell swore it wasn't on him.","prefix":"nd that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"","suffix":"\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is.","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"See ch.5: it was on him.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977658,"updatedAt":1794294200615,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294200615},{"id":"iRzW1j2ohORfjn-gtFZIf","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","quote":"like a pistol shot","prefix":": the fourth stair that cracked ","suffix":", the landing board that sighed,","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Pistols in Quell? Nothing else in the book has gunpowder. 'Like a whip' keeps the world clean.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553331,"updatedAt":1794293058463,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293058463},{"id":"iwy5e9Ok5GN8f83Y_jRxz","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","quote":"I saw her mast.","prefix":" her there myself on the tenth. ","suffix":"\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I do","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"The revision put 'mast' in both sentences. I've taken it out of the first.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977642,"updatedAt":1794294195266,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294195266},{"id":"jppOqpG1Cp1QSmYfbG12A","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","quote":"so many of them","prefix":"ince the autumn, and there were ","suffix":", hard and white and close, that","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Moon check, now the full moon is on the 7th: on the night of the 3rd it's four days off full and up until two or three in the morning. Bright enough to drown most of the stars. Clouds just clearing, a moon on the water? It's a better picture anyway, for a tower that throws light.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"rHph2pgGGdbftAM7bPNRf","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Took it: a moon four days off full laying a road on the water to the Teeth. And the moon, not the stars, when Hale dies.","createdAt":1794294191314}],"createdAt":1794293977644,"updatedAt":1794294204382,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794294204382},{"id":"l4KDwxzME41BVVPUUH0om","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"No *Gannet* on the fifteenth.","prefix":" the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n","suffix":" The entries for the fifteenth w","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"I've cut the 'Outbound 2 Rimefall' line above it: the bundle is Rimefall 8-21, so the 2nd isn't in it. If you want her to see the outbound entry too, she opens two bundles.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977661,"updatedAt":1794294201681,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294201681},{"id":"lTQ_DP-u2BRlROPf4m4Ew","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","quote":"the tide was making","prefix":"s leg. The wind had dropped and ","suffix":", the last big flood of the spri","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Right now, and lovely.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977665,"updatedAt":1794294203850,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294203850},{"id":"lW-XK-e5j7j6HW2QOU45U","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","quote":"up the town stair and then round","prefix":"r mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—","suffix":",\" Vane was saying. He sounded o","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"They went up the sea stair (Wren's instructions; Corin calling the gaps) and down it (Hale's). Mell's man was at the foot of the town stair. What did he see? If Vane has it wrong on purpose, good, but let us know it's wrong.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553333,"updatedAt":1794293058995,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293058995},{"id":"mXd3Zhxwpf8VZLqWBfT5t","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","quote":"Mell's men will bring you the tower's key","prefix":". It takes two, Agathe.\"\n\n\"Then ","suffix":", when the tower has had its nig","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Good, the vault holds now. But with these two lines added, Vane's 'Tonight?' read as a question about the tower. I've pointed it at the book. Or move these two lines after 'the seventh'.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794293977647,"updatedAt":1794294196867,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794294196867},{"id":"nYxctCTw0C9BmzTqLPiq7","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","quote":"Hobb lets me run messages","prefix":"t in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"","suffix":" up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren sa","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"How does Wren know the seal was taken? She came running with the tide book before Tamsin could speak, and nobody tells her on the page. One line in this chapter would set it up: Tamsin telling her, or Wren seeing the empty cord.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553343,"updatedAt":1794293054163,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293054163},{"id":"nsz1SZSwVASmZ1voLJExf","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"sealed, to be sent","prefix":"wn into the vault each quarter, ","suffix":" to the Margravine's treasury at","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"If the sheets go down once a quarter, this quarter's (the one with the 2nd and 25th of Rimefall in it) shouldn't be in the vault yet. At each spring tide?","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553344,"updatedAt":1794293054696,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293054696},{"id":"oX9YloBClj2Bex4dSYsaI","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","quote":"She said not to go on my own","prefix":"\"","suffix":",\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my o","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Where are we? The book runs on dates, so a phrase here (the morning of the 4th, after three hours' sleep) would help. And isn't she due at the Ledger Hall? Skipping work is a choice, and a costly one. Let her make it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553336,"updatedAt":1794293049312,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293049312},{"id":"t4nwU2OYZwRm0V6_l2TjB","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","quote":"Old Faraday","prefix":" had taught her the difference. ","suffix":", who had trained her, claimed h","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Is Faraday alive? Here he 'claims'; in ch.5 Vane says 'I liked your Mr Faraday' (past tense); in ch.6 his copying of this quarter wins the book. If he's dead or retired, a clause here would let the reader carry him.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553311,"updatedAt":1794293043395,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293043395},{"id":"tgcNdanhiRaiKFrCU6gf5","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","quote":"the way she had watched him understand everything","prefix":"it. She watched him understand, ","suffix":", for three years, through the g","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"I can't parse this. Do you mean that in three years she never once saw him at a loss? Worth another go.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553344,"updatedAt":1794293055254,"version":3,"madeOn":"the way she had watched him understand nothing","resolvedAt":1794293055254},{"id":"uasgsNeyUqBR1QG-d6sbI","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","quote":"the painted saints on the walls","prefix":"ps of the pews just showing and ","suffix":" up to their chests in the sea. ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Painted on the walls, so in ch.4 sc.3 what is 'the hollow of the painted St Brannoc's lifted hand'? A statue would do it, and it's a lovely image: a stone saint up to his chest holding the book above the water. I've cut 'painted' there for now.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553337,"updatedAt":1794293050944,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293050944},{"id":"v5yBLAYwOjOLQ7KBuKbLP","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","quote":"Couldn't — find it.","prefix":"the town stair. Wanted the key. ","suffix":" Then oil.\" He coughed again. \"I","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"A new hole. Agathe tells Vane 'Hale wears it round his neck'; Hale says they couldn't find it; a paragraph later Tamsin finds it on a cord round his neck. Where was it when they looked? If he'd hidden it and put it back on after they left, a line here would say so.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"_BkwtjxokKVPBEk_D_LHd","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Hale dropped it in the lamp oil when he heard them on the stair, and fished it out after; that's the burned hand. They came back with oil of their own.","createdAt":1794294192397}],"createdAt":1794293977654,"updatedAt":1794294204915,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794294204915},{"id":"xcTygF-7HNUgFdfHYfWKx","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"CPvxlKCtqtmDjgg7Nzt7F","quote":"Don't go out to the Flats on your own.","prefix":"y serious. \"I'll say this once. ","suffix":" Whatever you think you've found","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Who knows what (you asked): this is the one leak I don't think is earned. Nobody, Tamsin or reader, has any reason yet to think of the Flats; they come from Bessaly's letter, which nobody has read. If Iska knows about the Flats she knows the scheme, and ch.4 has her only half understanding it. Make the warning vaguer, or let her know more and show it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553327,"updatedAt":1794293057378,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794293057378},{"id":"ydALtDFLNvSKeK5nuFxf-","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","quote":"at a little after two in the morning","prefix":"it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, ","suffix":", with her nightgown tucked unde","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Jonah Pryce"},"body":"Timing: Hale says 'it's past midnight' as they leave, yet by a little after one Vane already knows they were up there an hour and came down, has got to Guild Row, and has been in for twenty minutes. Tight to impossible. Two o'clock?","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794286553332,"updatedAt":1794293047716,"version":3,"madeOn":"at a little after one in the morning","resolvedAt":1794293047716}],"revisionTasks":[{"id":"-wYm96YaXju6_SvIlzi4m","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Vane says Faraday missed the lie because he was \"always weeks behind\". But a slow copier would have copied the lie. Missing it means the inbound line was added after he'd copied that page, in other words backdated. That's a better story anyway: it gives you a second proof, with the lie found in the act of being made.","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":7,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293101906,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293101906},{"id":"2SsGgj3n3gFYL2kmP9dEJ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Tamsin tells Vane that copies without the inbound entry make it \"only her\". But copies without the entry prove the register was altered, and the alteration carries his stamp. I believe Vane opens the door because he's broken. I don't believe he does it because of this argument.","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":8,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293102441,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293102441},{"id":"3NMls6_L2QNctXx9oyEJ0","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"What I'd do: write the scheme down for yourself as a list of register entries, in order, with what each one is for. Then make Ruth's letter say exactly that, once, plainly, and make everything after it agree with it. Readers will forgive a lot of plot, but they won't forgive a climax that turns on a document they can't follow.","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":9,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293102975,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293102975},{"id":"4dA6BlQhkTnMh88_sLZVL","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The book is in close third on Tamsin, with two exceptions. The Iska scene in ch.3 works well, but it's the only one, so it reads as a device rather than a pattern. Either give Iska one more scene (the psalm swap, or the swim for the ledger in ch.6, would both be good) or accept it as a one-off. I'd lean towards one more. The other exception is the line about Mell being caught \"in the end... that was another week\". That's the narrator stepping outside Tamsin to tell us the future. I like how cool it is, but it's a different narrator from the rest of the book.","group":"Point of view","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"},{"kind":"character","id":"UO8tgDrebgYW6JXOkF4oP"},{"kind":"character","id":"uYI_7OMlY7Od3x6qkWJ4K"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":false,"order":29,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794286857952,"version":1},{"id":"6MkHop5Pp9eFxcmxgnppe","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"I don't think the arrest is rushed. Arrests are quick, and Tamsin's slip (\"That's not from the Gannet\") is exactly right: her cleverness is what convicts him. What's thin is that nobody acts. Corin goes without a fight, Tamsin watches, and the scene ends. Give her a failed attempt, even a small one (she follows them to the Tidegate and is turned away at the gate), and the scene will feel its proper weight without getting any longer.","group":"Pace: chapter 4 and chapter 6 (you asked)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"},{"kind":"character","id":"nkhC22Vro7E65XGijwWc_"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":26,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293112042,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293112042},{"id":"6vkzPDNCzhfA3hJyktQM1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Small habits I've marked once or twice and left you to carry through: past perfect where the time has moved back (\"than she had expected\"), a few comma splices in dialogue, and one \"didn't\" in narration that otherwise says \"did not\". Straight quotes are fine for now; a typesetter will curl them.","group":"Prose","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":false,"order":41,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794286857952,"version":1},{"id":"7y0ybzwll4bVINzXngT7_","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Is the Gannet a barge or a coaster?","group":"All of these are in the margin","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":false,"order":33,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794286857952,"version":1},{"id":"8HD-QGmL6hls9KxswTzlg","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Faraday now sits at the end of the Ledger Hall, so a reader will ask why Tamsin doesn't walk down and ask him about the entry. One line closes it.","group":"Two I've left for you","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce, second pass"},"done":true,"order":48,"createdAt":1794294136381,"updatedAt":1794294156468,"version":2,"doneAt":1794294156468},{"id":"9rdSYDNrYAMqgF5k9UP3O","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The boot came up on Wren's line \"a week ago\". It was four days. Changed.","group":"The things that don't fit that sequence (all marked in the margin)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"jKYRfxj4L7t7vuYAWSjL8"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":14,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293105623,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293105623},{"id":"A90ZPGQM0bAG6Eai1O0SZ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Vane says Agathe told him to make the entry \"in my office\". In chapter 3 she doesn't even open her own door. \"At Sorrel House\" squares it, unless her coming to him is the point.","group":"Two I've left for you","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"WRWnxWq5h68ViGtEwTlhA"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"},{"kind":"character","id":"bPnOI3lqgd0Tdit8oeWG_"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce, second pass"},"done":true,"order":49,"createdAt":1794294136381,"updatedAt":1794294156998,"version":2,"doneAt":1794294156998},{"id":"AVqvI9Uhh4lVN3kbjjAQ1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Then the climax depends on more of the same machinery: the Crown's copies in the vault, Faraday's copying, and Tamsin's argument that persuades Vane to open the door. Each of those has a snag (all three are queried in the margin):","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":5,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293100858,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293100858},{"id":"BLm8c2Ryy-sR8JmpHFr1n","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"You said you lean on \"looked\". There are 42 of them, and most are fine: \"He did not look at the register. He looked at her\" is the whole of that scene, so keep that kind. The ones to cut only move the camera (\"He looked at her.\" before a line of dialogue). I've cut or changed eight as examples and left the rest for you. The word you actually lean on is \"very\": 56 of them. I've trimmed a few as well, and you'll find more once you're looking.","group":"Prose","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":false,"order":39,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794286857952,"version":1},{"id":"FA5Q0GtoCtLiAhAUaSy6v","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"There's one more hole here, and readers will fall into it in chapter 6: the vault takes two keys. Agathe tells Vane to put the second book in the flood vault \"where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide\". But Hale holds the other key. How did Vane mean to open the door on the 7th? If Agathe's plan was for Mell to take Hale's key in the fire, that's a strong extra motive for the fire, and it should be on the page. As it stands, Vane walks down the stair on the 7th to do something he cannot do.","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"Ds8ANB9vN3KPE3_801S7p"},{"kind":"character","id":"bPnOI3lqgd0Tdit8oeWG_"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"},{"kind":"character","id":"-x4Rj3jYFrkHQNoj66tmq"},{"kind":"character","id":"uYI_7OMlY7Od3x6qkWJ4K"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":10,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293103509,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293103509},{"id":"H-bLrqBdINVaiy8uSegqI","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The fraud has to make sense on one sheet of paper (structure and plot logic)","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":0,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293098194,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293098194},{"id":"ISlhKOiih1f647idmq_Hf","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"How does Wren know Vane took the seal? She came running with the tide book before Tamsin could speak, and nobody tells her on the page.","group":"Who knows what, and when (you asked)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"jKYRfxj4L7t7vuYAWSjL8"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"},{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":24,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293110969,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293110969},{"id":"JDNYtyx20k7xSG2RtxYw6","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Vane reaches Sorrel House too soon after the tower visit for the man who watched it to have reported.","group":"The things that don't fit that sequence (all marked in the margin)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":15,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293106157,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293106157},{"id":"KFr2hCXLpFaOD3Rda6Iwm","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Breme: a home port, or the saltworks on Quell's own Flats?","group":"All of these are in the margin","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":false,"order":34,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794286857952,"version":1},{"id":"KgcKS8HMlvVr0FVcIku9w","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"6. Continuity, small but worth fixing","group":"Point of view","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":30,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293113642,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293113642},{"id":"NV9qceCYBZCfUXqSqAaR1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Agathe says \"Four days\" to the spring tide at one in the morning on the 4th. That makes it the 8th. \"Three days\" fixes it.","group":"The things that don't fit that sequence (all marked in the margin)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"bPnOI3lqgd0Tdit8oeWG_"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":11,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293104040,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293104040},{"id":"NxAFQj55b25GX6PJfRsih","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Chapter 5 has a bigger problem than chapter 4: why does Tamsin go to Vane? She walks into his office and hands him the whole case, tide books included, against Iska's advice. You know it's a mistake, because she thinks so herself in the street afterwards. But we never know what she meant to get by it. If it's to bargain for Corin, say so in a line before she goes in, and the reader will forgive her.","group":"Pace: chapter 4 and chapter 6 (you asked)","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"FjTsMQx8VjGj96N-iig05"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"04TQ6IPcgHyNMbV7CkFJn"},{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"},{"kind":"character","id":"UO8tgDrebgYW6JXOkF4oP"},{"kind":"character","id":"nkhC22Vro7E65XGijwWc_"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":27,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293112575,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293112575},{"id":"OZWfJyrmP4ZmaI61ybxHX","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"If the copies go down \"each quarter\", this quarter's copies shouldn't be in the vault yet.","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":6,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293101390,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293101390},{"id":"PN8O5ZtOcXzmhojyCfnhP","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Is Faraday alive? Vane speaks of him in the past tense.","group":"All of these are in the margin","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":32,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293114708,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293114708},{"id":"PWk8HJ4GAIXVvQb2fxJC3","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Iska's \"Don't go out to the Flats\" in ch.2. Nobody, reader or Tamsin, has any reason yet to think of the Flats. The idea comes from Ruth's letter, which nobody has read. Make the warning vaguer, or let Iska know more and show it.","group":"Who knows what, and when (you asked)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"UO8tgDrebgYW6JXOkF4oP"},{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":22,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293109892,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293109892},{"id":"SWAL9IcTzDVR5wobOHoDr","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"You say you think it wants to be longer. I'm not sure it does. At thirteen thousand words it's a complete story, and its economy is one of its virtues. If you do grow it, grow the middle. The day of the 6th, which Tamsin spends behind closed shutters, is a whole day the book skips; Corin's three days in the Tidegate happen offstage; and the court needs a fight if it stays. Don't add to the opening. It's right.","group":"ON LENGTH","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"},{"kind":"character","id":"nkhC22Vro7E65XGijwWc_"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":42,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293117375,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293117375},{"id":"ScpvM3bC8V1JCN1NjGRMG","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Ruth's letter (ch.3) says a ship is entered outbound, then entered as lost, and the Guild claims the rebate. The Gannet was to be \"entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later\" than the 2nd of Rimefall.","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":2,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293099258,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293099258},{"id":"UAZXZyFr9iJEkWj56_S3d","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Once the key and the vault details are settled, the structure is done. What's left is your own pass on \"looked\" and the \"and... and\" sentences in the slipway and the stair, then a copyedit. I'd be glad to do that copyedit when you're ready. It's a different job from this one, and cheaper.","group":"WHERE THAT LEAVES IT","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce, second pass"},"done":false,"order":50,"createdAt":1794294136381,"updatedAt":1794294136381,"version":1},{"id":"WDtAhMW-cUlVZwFi3tREK","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Two wave-shaped keys (Agathe's and Hale's). Is that deliberate?","group":"All of these are in the margin","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"bPnOI3lqgd0Tdit8oeWG_"},{"kind":"character","id":"-x4Rj3jYFrkHQNoj66tmq"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":38,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293116841,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293116841},{"id":"aCV0SUOMlKv_ltMOnXj2L","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The \"and... and...\" sentences are your music. In the quiet scenes they're lovely. In the running scenes (the slipway at Cray's End, the stair during the fire) they slow the eye just when it should speed up. Try breaking one or two there.","group":"Prose","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":false,"order":40,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794286857952,"version":1},{"id":"b9Z5WtHSV_aFQYZaB_xre","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Mell finds the bell in Corin's coat straight away. If the Cray's End foreman reported that a man took something, say so, and Mell's search becomes a hunt rather than luck.","group":"Who knows what, and when (you asked)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"uYI_7OMlY7Od3x6qkWJ4K"},{"kind":"character","id":"nkhC22Vro7E65XGijwWc_"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":25,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293111507,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293111507},{"id":"bAfw7AzXv0whC-UUadNXZ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Swimming: Tamsin \"learned to swim before she learned to read\" and hides the ledger \"where only a swimmer could reach it\". Then Iska has to swim for it \"because Tamsin could not have reached it alone\".","group":"All of these are in the margin","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"},{"kind":"character","id":"UO8tgDrebgYW6JXOkF4oP"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":36,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293115775,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293115775},{"id":"bYDQcHaXc423CloQMk04b","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"This is the most important point in the letter, and it's the hinge of the book. As I read it, the reader is told three slightly different versions of the scheme:","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":1,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293098725,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293098725},{"id":"dCYCdAMU1Zl8OTN0YwgFn","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"This is new. Agathe tells Vane that Hale \"wears it round his neck\". Hale, dying, says Mell's men \"wanted the key. Couldn't find it.\" A paragraph later Tamsin finds it on a cord round his neck. In the vault Vane says \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\" Mell's men were told where to look, so a reader will ask why they didn't. If Hale hid it when he heard them on the stair and put it back on after they'd gone, one clause in his dying speech covers it. Otherwise move it somewhere they wouldn't look.","group":"Hale's key (ch.3, 5 and 6)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"bPnOI3lqgd0Tdit8oeWG_"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"},{"kind":"character","id":"-x4Rj3jYFrkHQNoj66tmq"},{"kind":"character","id":"uYI_7OMlY7Od3x6qkWJ4K"},{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce, second pass"},"done":true,"order":44,"createdAt":1794294136381,"updatedAt":1794294154337,"version":2,"doneAt":1794294154337},{"id":"doTu0gGM5sjDQIgrjHuTI","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"In ch.6 Vane has been \"up all night\" with the justiciar's clerk, but at dawn he was in the vault with Tamsin. If the court is on the 8th, say so in the scene's first line and that knot comes undone.","group":"The things that don't fit that sequence (all marked in the margin)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"},{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":16,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293106691,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293106691},{"id":"eLcVySCTYCozoSLyReqWj","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Is Tamsin a clerk or an assessor? Your note says junior assessor. The book says both, and the seal depends on it.","group":"All of these are in the margin","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":31,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293114175,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293114175},{"id":"el0g3oc6PtSR-P6MyeYuN","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Chapter 6 resolves too neatly, as you suspected. The real climax is the vault, and it's good. The Tide Court is a report: Pell compares two books, Vane confesses in one line, Agathe says one fine sentence and nothing else, and Mell is caught in a flash-forward. You asked what I'd cut first, and this is it, as it stands. I'd either cut the court to a page or two that Tamsin hears about, or make it a real fight. Agathe is your best antagonist and she goes down without a word. Let her argue. She keeps a ledger \"like any merchant\", and her version of the scheme could sound almost reasonable to a justiciar who dislikes Quell. Let the tide book win against something.","group":"Pace: chapter 4 and chapter 6 (you asked)","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"Ds8ANB9vN3KPE3_801S7p"},{"kind":"character","id":"fqMuxvZb5b_D5Z1audRJp"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"},{"kind":"character","id":"bPnOI3lqgd0Tdit8oeWG_"},{"kind":"character","id":"uYI_7OMlY7Od3x6qkWJ4K"},{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":28,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293113108,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293113108},{"id":"gr5CS0p1iZVFhYhN510G6","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Hale's \"day after tomorrow\" at two in the morning on the 6th is, strictly, the 8th.","group":"The things that don't fit that sequence (all marked in the margin)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-x4Rj3jYFrkHQNoj66tmq"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":12,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293104573,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293104573},{"id":"j2Ol3IS9kLpxBDAlHlNRH","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Who told Mell to watch the tower? Did someone follow Wren up there? One line would do it.","group":"Who knows what, and when (you asked)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"uYI_7OMlY7Od3x6qkWJ4K"},{"kind":"character","id":"jKYRfxj4L7t7vuYAWSjL8"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":23,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293110425,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293110425},{"id":"l3HhlsbMov5xPzKz7FtP_","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The fix is to choose your anchor and move the other dates to match it. The spring tide on the 7th is the one the plot can't do without, so keep that. Then the neap that Hale's proof needs falls around the 29th or 30th of Rimefall, or around the 14th or 15th. So move the false inbound entry to one of those dates. It's a change of a few words in ch.1, 3, 5 and 6. And drop \"the dark of last moon\", or move the wreck to match. Then do the ch.6 tides: high water at dawn means low water around noon. So Iska can't swim the nave \"that morning at low water\", and at four in the afternoon the tide is coming in, not going out. If you keep the tide tables beside you as Hale does, you'll catch these yourself.","group":"The days, as I read them","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-x4Rj3jYFrkHQNoj66tmq"},{"kind":"character","id":"UO8tgDrebgYW6JXOkF4oP"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":21,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293109358,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293109358},{"id":"laO_h_bmA08zdmdqO_-Vs","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Hale's proof needs a neap on the 25th of Rimefall. That fits: neaps about a week before the next new moon.","group":"The days, as I read them","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-x4Rj3jYFrkHQNoj66tmq"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":19,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293108293,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293108293},{"id":"ooHznw9L-AAj6VoiAhbGy","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"In ch.1 the freshly gilded names belong to \"two Guild barges\", not the Constance. Gild her there, or drop the gilding at Cray's End.","group":"Two I've left for you","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce, second pass"},"done":true,"order":46,"createdAt":1794294136381,"updatedAt":1794294155399,"version":2,"doneAt":1794294155399},{"id":"qJTyZug0RA1iuolg3eZnH","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"\"Constant\" among the nameboards and the \"Constance\" on the mud in ch.1. If that's deliberate it's a superb clue and Tamsin must catch it. If not, rename one.","group":"All of these are in the margin","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":35,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293115243,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293115243},{"id":"sBPsmoOw5_Gco1m3jyR1T","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"With the full moon on the 7th, the night of the 3rd has a moon four days off full, up until two or three in the morning. Bright enough to put out most of those stars over the sea stair. It's an easy change, and a moon on the water might suit a scene about a lighthouse better anyway.","group":"Two I've left for you","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce, second pass"},"done":true,"order":47,"createdAt":1794294136381,"updatedAt":1794294155932,"version":2,"doneAt":1794294155932},{"id":"tHTetn9fNYO46Ray4nLfc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The painted St Brannoc has a \"lifted hand\" with a hollow in it. A statue would solve that, and it's a lovely image.","group":"All of these are in the margin","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":37,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293116307,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293116307},{"id":"tV8t8Q_5KL89ae31s8HYo","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"But then the next new moon, and the next springs, fall around the 2nd or 3rd of Thawmonth, which is now, in chapter 1. Corin's \"the stern's out of the water at the lowest springs\" and the bright stars on the 3rd both agree with that. The spring tide on the 7th, which the whole back half waits for, would fall near the next neaps, and the vault wouldn't open.","group":"The days, as I read them","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"e3ONzTt5um_G9tKvTb0Cl"},{"kind":"character","id":"nkhC22Vro7E65XGijwWc_"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":20,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293108825,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293108825},{"id":"tvIGg7s-VALI77AznUZZZ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Ruth also says they always add an inbound entry afterwards, \"to show the cargo was never landed\". I can't make this work. If a ship is entered inbound, wasn't its cargo landed? Why does an inbound entry make the rebate \"clean\"?","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":3,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293099792,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293099792},{"id":"u0aHpgFYlOobH0kKlalMR","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"Corin has \"a week's beard\" after not quite three days. I've changed it to three days'.","group":"The things that don't fit that sequence (all marked in the margin)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"nkhC22Vro7E65XGijwWc_"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":13,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293105089,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293105089},{"id":"uFLD7PuRo8DBNxoMS4k2m","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The tides are harder. Spring tides come with the new moon and the full moon, about every fifteen days, and neaps fall halfway between. Readers will assume Quell's moon works like ours unless you tell them otherwise. As written:","group":"The days, as I read them","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":17,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293107227,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293107227},{"id":"vRMPGjU-9h-yKDpXdPY6T","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The Gannet went down at the dark of the moon, on or about the 2nd of Rimefall.","group":"The days, as I read them","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":18,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293107758,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293107758},{"id":"viI-drRv6YzBwj4PBQeGu","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"IN ORDER","group":"ON LENGTH","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":43,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293117909,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293117909},{"id":"vlpC7j7vcEV3EF9HyMNQh","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"If the sheets go down at every spring tide and the door takes two keys, someone from the tower came down that stair every fortnight. Presumably Hale. Say so in a sentence. It would also tell Vane exactly whom to expect on the 7th, and it leaves one question: if he expects the key-holder, why does he come without Mell? I believe he would; let one of them say why.","group":"Hale's key (ch.3, 5 and 6)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-x4Rj3jYFrkHQNoj66tmq"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"},{"kind":"character","id":"uYI_7OMlY7Od3x6qkWJ4K"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce, second pass"},"done":true,"order":45,"createdAt":1794294136381,"updatedAt":1794294154866,"version":2,"doneAt":1794294154866},{"id":"xQ1U9TsU_dP37sa1ibokk","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","text":"The register Tamsin reads (ch.1) has outbound on the 2nd, inbound on the 25th, and no loss yet. Vane says the Guild will claim the rebate \"in due course\".","group":"WHAT MOST NEEDS WORK","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"K6RGUXaVAtbK0IPqS0YrN"},{"kind":"character","id":"qTauDAEKEqUX7Wev0_d-c"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Jonah Pryce"},"done":true,"order":4,"createdAt":1794286857952,"updatedAt":1794293100325,"version":2,"doneAt":1794293100325}],"continuitySuppressions":[],"writingLogs":[{"id":"PN2B4I5suF0CpXubpNHls","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","date":"2026-11-10","words":13789,"createdAt":1794283488199,"updatedAt":1794294038807}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"-h1yXxJJ1Gl-4KJkz0nS9","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—followed the mudlark up there this afternoon, and sat under the town stair to see who came,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"They came at midnight, the girl and the cripple, round by the sea stair. They were up there an hour. Then they went down the same way.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your assessor. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"And the tower's key. It takes two, Agathe.\"\n\n\"Then Mell's men will bring you the tower's key, when the tower has had its night. Hale wears it round his neck. He won't be needing it.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":826,"createdAt":1794287234163},{"id":"0qg_4nnudguk-Zg3KezJQ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet* was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the fifteenth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning. The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in her palm and closed her fingers over it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1059,"createdAt":1794287211427},{"id":"0u8arXflyhCadMQt2kHoG","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied around with tarred twine. \"She went down at the dark of last moon. A month gone. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the lowest springs. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest.\" He looked at her. \"And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":969,"createdAt":1794286685772},{"id":"0uyRGkuQ7vub1xjaBX8Tc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1689,"createdAt":1794287217456},{"id":"0y3RR4a5Z3TXSHaxFy_v3","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor. She had lain awake working it out. Vane wanted her quiet, and Mell would do what Vane told him. If she could make Vane afraid enough of what she knew, he might buy her quiet with Corin.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer had entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I like your Mr Faraday, and he likes you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1048,"createdAt":1794287255336},{"id":"0yhuJ-_FvTgvDpsmIGiur","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor's mark, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1051,"createdAt":1794286689570},{"id":"1dCGOqHMRw5hT5gA4YhLA","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1599,"createdAt":1794287212920},{"id":"22XBhkJugNApdjfzsHpov","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel. She had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made a week later says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, in my office, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she raised his eyes to him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with three days' beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line a week ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1361,"createdAt":1794287773067},{"id":"2XZPmEZp8-YUNFdxRAtsf","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her. She had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed — she couldn't help it — and Iska stared at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":876,"createdAt":1794287241749},{"id":"30RJauDW0nTuyyvRFm1aP","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel. She had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made a week later says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, in my office, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she looked at him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with a week's beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line a week ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1359,"createdAt":1794287434541},{"id":"35YheBpNW8YoV-k3fykI1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out in ballast on the second of Rimefall, bring her round to the Flats and leave her there. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will write the Gannet in again after, laden, as if she had come home, because they must, or there is no cargo to lose. They will put it in among old entries, so it looks old. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The fifteenth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the fifteenth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the fifteenth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1681,"createdAt":1794287225002},{"id":"3EfS8NZFcnPEDmkqTiuBL","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand nothing, for three years, through the glass door; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1211,"createdAt":1794287261424},{"id":"3FfSjxBZ0RYq1el8Y5vTM","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the start of Rimefall, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1048,"createdAt":1794287199293},{"id":"3bWvbhAsFHphl3L_qs2b5","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, for the same reason and in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets that went down into the vault each quarter, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand nothing, for three years, through the glass door; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down very slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1208,"createdAt":1794286714269},{"id":"49ib2LYUQV4nd0BfpgVWg","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Wanted the key. Couldn't — find it. Then oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Day after tomorrow. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the stars over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"","wordCount":1033,"createdAt":1794287258343},{"id":"4CzU1pXm_TSU274DXsL5K","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea, and in the middle of the nave a stone St Brannoc standing in the water to his chest, with one hand lifted as if to keep something dry. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her. She had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go looking on your own.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed — she couldn't help it — and Iska stared at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":905,"createdAt":1794287244740},{"id":"59KY9DpWchdSgvVYU3UXx","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"CPvxlKCtqtmDjgg7Nzt7F","text":"It began to rain as she crossed the Saltmarket, a thin cold rain coming in sideways off the water, and by the time she reached Guild Row the hem of her skirt was black to the knee.\n\nSorrel House stood at the top of the Row where the street widened into a little square with a dry fountain in it. It was the oldest of the Guild houses and the only one still built of timber, black oak beams and white plaster, three storeys leaning out over the street so that the upper windows nearly touched the house across the way. People said the Sorrels had refused to rebuild in stone after the Great Flood because stone was for people who meant to leave. Tamsin had always thought that a strange thing to be proud of, but then she had never understood the Guild.\n\nA manservant took her coat and the folder and showed her up to a room on the first floor that smelled of beeswax and peat smoke. The windows were small and leaded and let in almost nothing of the grey afternoon. There was a fire, at least. There was a fire, and in front of it, in a high-backed chair with a rug over her knees, was Mother Agathe Sorrel.\n\nShe was a small woman, much smaller than Tamsin remembered from the Guild processions, and very old. Her hair was white and pulled back so tight it seemed to stretch the skin of her forehead. She wore black, as Guild mothers did, and on every finger of both hands she wore a ring, heavy old rings of gold and jet and green stone that clicked against each other when she moved. Her left eye was clouded milk-white. The right one was dark and quick and missed nothing.\n\n\"The Exchequer's girl,\" she said. \"Sit down. You're dripping on my floor.\"\n\nTamsin sat on the stool she was pointed to. The manservant brought the folder and gave it to Mother Agathe, who did not open it.\n\n\"The quarter's assessment, Mother,\" Tamsin said. \"The Chief Assessor sends his compliments. The Guild's hull duty for the quarter comes to four hundred and twelve crowns, less the rebates already allowed—\"\n\n\"I can read, child. I've been reading Exchequer assessments since before your mother was born.\" Agathe laid the folder on her knees and put one ringed hand flat on top of it. \"What is your name?\"\n\n\"Orrey, Mother. Tamsin Orrey.\"\n\n\"Orrey. From the Quarter.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\"\n\n\"Your father was a lighterman.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Drowned, wasn't he? In the year of the bad ice.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\" Tamsin kept her voice level. She was not sure what was happening, but she was sure that something was.\n\n\"It's a hard trade,\" Agathe said. \"The water takes what it's owed. My husband used to say that the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant, and it always collects.\" The dark eye moved over Tamsin's face, unhurried. \"I hear you've been asking about the *Gannet*.\"\n\nThe fire cracked. Somewhere below them in the house a door closed.\n\n\"I noticed an entry in the register, Mother,\" Tamsin said carefully. \"I mentioned it to the Chief Assessor. It was nothing.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"He said it was nothing.\"\n\n\"Then I expect it was.\" Agathe smiled. Her teeth were small and brown and very even. \"Edric is a sound man. He's been a good friend to this house for twenty years. If he tells you a thing is nothing, child, you may rest your head on it.\" She tapped the folder. \"Four hundred and twelve, you say.\"\n\n\"Less rebates, Mother.\"\n\n\"Less rebates.\" She turned her head toward the door. \"Iska. See Miss Orrey out. And give her something hot before she goes. She's wet through. We don't want the Exchequer saying we send its clerks home to catch their deaths.\"\n\nTamsin had not seen anyone else in the room. But a young woman stood up from the window seat at the far end, where the shadows were deepest, and came forward. She was perhaps twenty-two, tall, with her mother's dark eyes, both of them clear, and a long plain face that might have been handsome if she had not looked so tired. She wore the black of the Guild, but she wore it badly, with ink on the cuffs.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said to Tamsin. \"Kitchen's warmest.\"\n\nThey went down two flights of narrow stairs. Iska Sorrel did not speak on the way, and did not take her to the kitchen. She took her to the side door, where the manservant was waiting with Tamsin's coat, and she took the coat from him and sent him away and held it out herself.\n\n\"You're not getting anything hot,\" she said quietly, \"you're getting out of this house. My mother likes you.\"\n\n\"That's good, isn't it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Iska glanced at the door the manservant had gone through, and then back at Tamsin. Up close her face was very young and very serious. \"I'll say this once. Don't go out to the Flats on your own. Whatever you think you've found, don't go and look at it on your own.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"Then good.\" Iska opened the door. The rain was coming down hard now, hammering on the cobbles of the little square, filling the dry fountain. \"Go home, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nTamsin went out into the rain. When she turned at the corner of the square to look back, the side door was shut and there was a light in the window seat on the first floor, and a shape in it, very small and very still.","wordCount":940,"createdAt":1794286730936},{"id":"5wA19kulfakrrXD12BO2_","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets that went down into the vault each quarter, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand nothing, for three years, through the glass door; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1202,"createdAt":1794287259834},{"id":"66pzwDOvZy4pOI6pSpq65","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Day after tomorrow. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the stars over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"","wordCount":1025,"createdAt":1794287256869},{"id":"6IM-mhSa7SK5ogq6hS7Zq","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—followed the mudlark up there this afternoon, and sat under the town stair to see who came,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"They came at midnight, the girl and the cripple, round by the sea stair. They were up there an hour. Then they went down the same way.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your assessor. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"And the tower's key. It takes two, Agathe.\"\n\n\"Then Mell's men will bring you the tower's key, when the tower has had its night. Hale wears it round his neck. He won't be needing it.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"The spring tide. Three days: the seventh. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of a scallop shell, to match the lock.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck four. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":834,"createdAt":1794294031836},{"id":"6RAtI8ozoT3RKTfKEZ9Sj","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":" C\n[\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey\n]\n\n B\n[\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey\n]\n\n Count Two\n[\n  Place: #The Long Quay\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask\n]\n\n","wordCount":25,"createdAt":1794283701251},{"id":"77tZv7AdEFqdwRoOslnWW","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with the Chief Assessor's mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the last voyage. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor's mark, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.\n\n# Sorrel House\n\n[\n  Place: #Sorrel House\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Agathe Sorrel @@Iska Sorrel\n  When: same day\n  Relationships: ~Agathe Sorrel/Iska Sorrel:Mother and daughter\n]\n\nIt began to rain as she crossed the Saltmarket, a thin cold rain coming in sideways off the water, and by the time she reached Guild Row the hem of her skirt was black to the knee.\n\nSorrel House stood at the top of the Row where the street widened into a little square with a dry fountain in it. It was the oldest of the Guild houses and the only one still built of timber, black oak beams and white plaster, three storeys leaning out over the street so that the upper windows nearly touched the house across the way. People said the Sorrels had refused to rebuild in stone after the Great Flood because stone was for people who meant to leave. Tamsin had always thought that a strange thing to be proud of, but then she had never understood the Guild.\n\nA manservant took her coat and the folder and showed her up to a room on the first floor that smelled of beeswax and peat smoke. The windows were small and leaded and let in almost nothing of the grey afternoon. There was a fire, at least. There was a fire, and in front of it, in a high-backed chair with a rug over her knees, was Mother Agathe Sorrel.\n\nShe was a small woman, much smaller than Tamsin remembered from the Guild processions, and very old. Her hair was white and pulled back so tight it seemed to stretch the skin of her forehead. She wore black, as Guild mothers did, and on every finger of both hands she wore a ring, heavy old rings of gold and jet and green stone that clicked against each other when she moved. Her left eye was clouded milk-white. The right one was dark and quick and missed nothing.\n\n\"The Exchequer's girl,\" she said. \"Sit down. You're dripping on my floor.\"\n\nTamsin sat on the stool she was pointed to. The manservant brought the folder and gave it to Mother Agathe, who did not open it.\n\n\"The quarter's assessment, Mother,\" Tamsin said. \"The Chief Assessor sends his compliments. The Guild's hull duty for the quarter comes to four hundred and twelve crowns, less the rebates already allowed—\"\n\n\"I can read, child. I've been reading Exchequer assessments since before your mother was born.\" Agathe laid the folder on her knees and put one ringed hand flat on top of it. \"What is your name?\"\n\n\"Orrey, Mother. Tamsin Orrey.\"\n\n\"Orrey. From the Quarter.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\"\n\n\"Your father was a lighterman.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Drowned, wasn't he? In the year of the bad ice.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\" Tamsin kept her voice level. She was not sure what was happening, but she was sure that something was.\n\n\"It's a hard trade,\" Agathe said. \"The water takes what it's owed. My husband used to say that the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant, and it always collects.\" The dark eye moved over Tamsin's face, unhurried. \"I hear you've been asking about the *Gannet*.\"\n\nThe fire cracked. Somewhere below them in the house a door closed.\n\n\"I noticed an entry in the register, Mother,\" Tamsin said carefully. \"I mentioned it to the Chief Assessor. It was nothing.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"He said it was nothing.\"\n\n\"Then I expect it was.\" Agathe smiled. Her teeth were small and brown and very even. \"Edric is a sound man. He's been a good friend to this house for twenty years. If he tells you a thing is nothing, child, you may rest your head on it.\" She tapped the folder. \"Four hundred and twelve, you say.\"\n\n\"Less rebates, Mother.\"\n\n\"Less rebates.\" She turned her head toward the door. \"Iska. See Miss Orrey out. And give her something hot before she goes, she's wet through. We don't want the Exchequer saying we send its clerks home to catch their deaths.\"\n\nTamsin had not seen anyone else in the room. But a young woman stood up from the window seat at the far end, where the shadows were deepest, and came forward. She was perhaps twenty-two, tall, with her mother's dark eyes, both of them clear, and a long plain face that might have been handsome if she had not looked so tired. She wore the black of the Guild, but she wore it badly, with ink on the cuffs.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said to Tamsin. \"Kitchen's warmest.\"\n\nThey went down two flights of narrow stairs. Iska Sorrel did not speak on the way, and did not take her to the kitchen. She took her to the side door, where the manservant was waiting with Tamsin's coat, and she took the coat from him and sent him away and held it out herself.\n\n\"You're not getting anything hot,\" she said quietly, \"you're getting out of this house. My mother likes you.\"\n\n\"That's good, isn't it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Iska looked at the door the manservant had gone through, and then back at Tamsin. Up close her face was very young and very serious. \"I'll say this once. Don't go out to the Flats on your own. Whatever you think you've found, don't go and look at it on your own.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"Then good.\" Iska opened the door. The rain was coming down hard now, hammering on the cobbles of the little square, filling the dry fountain. \"Go home, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nTamsin went out into the rain. When she turned at the corner of the square to look back, the side door was shut and there was a light in the window seat on the first floor, and a shape in it, very small and very still.","wordCount":2021,"createdAt":1794283945167},{"id":"7UFzTVJUQMIbCMr7nyGAg","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt was the morning of the fourth. She had slept three hours, and the Ledger Hall had expected her at eight. She had decided on the walk down to the Quarter not to go, and had not let herself think about what that would cost. It had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She stopped there too, and brought the lantern close. The *Constance* had been on the mud that morning, her name freshly gilded. One letter, and a coat of paint. She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet, the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1118,"createdAt":1794294035000},{"id":"8LQOn7nZyfYwsy9e8mvQc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc's that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray. Tamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her, and she had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed, she couldn't help it, and Iska looked at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":875,"createdAt":1794286704769},{"id":"8eHGsmQUycpnevc5HgUAu","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":" The Low-Tide Count\n[\n  Place: #The Long Quay\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask\n]\n\n","wordCount":14,"createdAt":1794283534330},{"id":"8x9eqlRgtFysMEi0kKKbI","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and very short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But Vane knows. Somebody told him I'd been asking about the *Gannet*, and I don't know who, because the only person I asked was him. And then Mother Agathe knew, three hours later. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went very still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.\n","wordCount":1074,"createdAt":1794283976128},{"id":"9FkxW9iGKnM-Bu1xDML6p","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the last voyage. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor's mark, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1055,"createdAt":1794286688303},{"id":"9Jowka1pV9kuWOjWhBsR_","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets in old Faraday's hand that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could. For forty years, at every spring tide, it had been Brother Hale who came down this stair with the tower's key and stood at this door beside whoever was Chief Assessor while the sheets went in.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the fortnight she wanted in a minute, *Rimefall 8–21, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second*, and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\nNo *Gannet* on the fifteenth. The entries for the fifteenth were all there, the *Marigold* and two Guild barges and a coaster out of Hesk, in Faraday's slow careful hand, and the line that sat so close above them in the green book upstairs was not among them.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copies on the day,\" he said. \"He always has. He's the only man in the building who does.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1319,"createdAt":1794294089396},{"id":"9VTKgWz03d-VW3falEf88","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her. She had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed, she couldn't help it, and Iska looked at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":874,"createdAt":1794286706668},{"id":"9i6WLMxjzlxW4z0aUIkkO","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":779,"createdAt":1794287228018},{"id":"A50OJjMt9PclDSktI_Vho","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the fortnight she wanted in a minute, *Rimefall 8–21, sealed at the springs of the 22nd*, and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1274,"createdAt":1794287270570},{"id":"AZBWOiFq9DiFTk6SzzU-G","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, very precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book — that it was gone. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had told him about the tide books. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1022,"createdAt":1794286709836},{"id":"B77SCHdHBHtBn0STe-aFj","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets in old Faraday's hand that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the fortnight she wanted in a minute, *Rimefall 8–21, sealed at the springs of the 22nd*, and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No *Gannet* on the fifteenth. The entries for the fifteenth were all there, the *Marigold* and two Guild barges and a coaster out of Hesk, in Faraday's slow careful hand, and the line that sat so close above them in the green book upstairs was not among them.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copies on the day,\" he said. \"He always has. He's the only man in the building who does.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1295,"createdAt":1794294038168},{"id":"BEA5s8s6npJ8h-ziD5Uh5","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with the Chief Assessor's mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the last voyage. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor's mark, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.\n\n# Sorrel House\n\n## Assessment at Sorrel House\n[\n  Place: #Sorrel House\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Agathe Sorrel @@Iska Sorrel\n  When: same day\n  Relationships: ~Agathe Sorrel/Iska Sorrel:Mother and daughter\n]\n\nIt began to rain as she crossed the Saltmarket, a thin cold rain coming in sideways off the water, and by the time she reached Guild Row the hem of her skirt was black to the knee.\n\nSorrel House stood at the top of the Row where the street widened into a little square with a dry fountain in it. It was the oldest of the Guild houses and the only one still built of timber, black oak beams and white plaster, three storeys leaning out over the street so that the upper windows nearly touched the house across the way. People said the Sorrels had refused to rebuild in stone after the Great Flood because stone was for people who meant to leave. Tamsin had always thought that a strange thing to be proud of, but then she had never understood the Guild.\n\nA manservant took her coat and the folder and showed her up to a room on the first floor that smelled of beeswax and peat smoke. The windows were small and leaded and let in almost nothing of the grey afternoon. There was a fire, at least. There was a fire, and in front of it, in a high-backed chair with a rug over her knees, was Mother Agathe Sorrel.\n\nShe was a small woman, much smaller than Tamsin remembered from the Guild processions, and very old. Her hair was white and pulled back so tight it seemed to stretch the skin of her forehead. She wore black, as Guild mothers did, and on every finger of both hands she wore a ring, heavy old rings of gold and jet and green stone that clicked against each other when she moved. Her left eye was clouded milk-white. The right one was dark and quick and missed nothing.\n\n\"The Exchequer's girl,\" she said. \"Sit down. You're dripping on my floor.\"\n\nTamsin sat on the stool she was pointed to. The manservant brought the folder and gave it to Mother Agathe, who did not open it.\n\n\"The quarter's assessment, Mother,\" Tamsin said. \"The Chief Assessor sends his compliments. The Guild's hull duty for the quarter comes to four hundred and twelve crowns, less the rebates already allowed—\"\n\n\"I can read, child. I've been reading Exchequer assessments since before your mother was born.\" Agathe laid the folder on her knees and put one ringed hand flat on top of it. \"What is your name?\"\n\n\"Orrey, Mother. Tamsin Orrey.\"\n\n\"Orrey. From the Quarter.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\"\n\n\"Your father was a lighterman.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Drowned, wasn't he? In the year of the bad ice.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\" Tamsin kept her voice level. She was not sure what was happening, but she was sure that something was.\n\n\"It's a hard trade,\" Agathe said. \"The water takes what it's owed. My husband used to say that the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant, and it always collects.\" The dark eye moved over Tamsin's face, unhurried. \"I hear you've been asking about the *Gannet*.\"\n\nThe fire cracked. Somewhere below them in the house a door closed.\n\n\"I noticed an entry in the register, Mother,\" Tamsin said carefully. \"I mentioned it to the Chief Assessor. It was nothing.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"He said it was nothing.\"\n\n\"Then I expect it was.\" Agathe smiled. Her teeth were small and brown and very even. \"Edric is a sound man. He's been a good friend to this house for twenty years. If he tells you a thing is nothing, child, you may rest your head on it.\" She tapped the folder. \"Four hundred and twelve, you say.\"\n\n\"Less rebates, Mother.\"\n\n\"Less rebates.\" She turned her head toward the door. \"Iska. See Miss Orrey out. And give her something hot before she goes, she's wet through. We don't want the Exchequer saying we send its clerks home to catch their deaths.\"\n\nTamsin had not seen anyone else in the room. But a young woman stood up from the window seat at the far end, where the shadows were deepest, and came forward. She was perhaps twenty-two, tall, with her mother's dark eyes, both of them clear, and a long plain face that might have been handsome if she had not looked so tired. She wore the black of the Guild, but she wore it badly, with ink on the cuffs.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said to Tamsin. \"Kitchen's warmest.\"\n\nThey went down two flights of narrow stairs. Iska Sorrel did not speak on the way, and did not take her to the kitchen. She took her to the side door, where the manservant was waiting with Tamsin's coat, and she took the coat from him and sent him away and held it out herself.\n\n\"You're not getting anything hot,\" she said quietly, \"you're getting out of this house. My mother likes you.\"\n\n\"That's good, isn't it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Iska looked at the door the manservant had gone through, and then back at Tamsin. Up close her face was very young and very serious. \"I'll say this once. Don't go out to the Flats on your own. Whatever you think you've found, don't go and look at it on your own.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"Then good.\" Iska opened the door. The rain was coming down hard now, hammering on the cobbles of the little square, filling the dry fountain. \"Go home, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nTamsin went out into the rain. When she turned at the corner of the square to look back, the side door was shut and there was a light in the window seat on the first floor, and a shape in it, very small and very still.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Patience\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask @@Wren\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The register shows the Gannet inbound after she sank:@Corin Dask\n]\n\nThe *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and very short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But Vane knows. Somebody told him I'd been asking about the *Gannet*, and I don't know who, because the only person I asked was him. And then Mother Agathe knew, three hours later. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went very still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.\n","wordCount":3125,"createdAt":1794283945123},{"id":"C0sSWSD4dJVCZOLbF94xb","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet* was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning. The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in her palm and closed her fingers over it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1059,"createdAt":1794287209947},{"id":"C8t-2c_spDoVnzDLSn4vc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel.\n\nShe had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made a week later says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, in my office, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she looked at him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with three days' beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line five days ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1359,"createdAt":1794294040067},{"id":"C9Cvi6DrNE4Hb5ppKIzQ2","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt was the morning of the fourth. She had slept three hours, and the Ledger Hall had expected her at eight. She had decided on the walk down to the Quarter not to go, and had not let herself think about what that would cost. It had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She stopped there too, with the lantern held close. The *Constance* had been on the mud that morning, her name freshly gilded. One letter, and a coat of paint. She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet, the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1118,"createdAt":1794294034367},{"id":"CvooLyv9MI0Ta1DsSDKyt","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, for the same reason and in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets that went down into the vault each quarter, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand nothing, for three years, through the glass door; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down very slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1208,"createdAt":1794286713635},{"id":"DADt8qYOfT-OGUI8_GhKw","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied with tarred twine. \"She went down at the dark of last moon. A month gone. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the lowest springs. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest.\" He looked at her. \"And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":968,"createdAt":1794286686403},{"id":"DRd-US7_cGZp5xoZDcmYp","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1678,"createdAt":1794287214437},{"id":"DpDw6N0MXIXD6HtmlZO37","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—followed the mudlark up there this afternoon, and sat under the town stair to see who came,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"They came at midnight, the girl and the cripple, round by the sea stair. They were up there an hour. Then they went down the same way.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your assessor. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"And the tower's key. It takes two, Agathe.\"\n\n\"Then Mell's men will bring you the tower's key, when the tower has had its night. Hale wears it round his neck. He won't be needing it.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"The spring tide. Three days: the seventh. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":828,"createdAt":1794287235682},{"id":"ESRyVVdAyfLLh5aV2idxT","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a terrible tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Day after tomorrow. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the stars over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"\n\n# Spring Tide\n\n## Under the Exchequer\n[\n  Place: #The flood vault\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Edric Vane\n  When: +1 day\n  Facts: ?Iska has the second book:@Edric Vane\n]\n\nThe spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, for the same reason and in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets that went down into the vault each quarter, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand nothing, for three years, through the glass door; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down very slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Tide Court\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Iska Sorrel @@Agathe Sorrel @@Edric Vane @@Justiciar Pell @@Corin Dask @@Wren\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The second book:@Iska Sorrel\n]\n\nThe Tide Court sat at noon in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the front, at the long table where the witnesses stood, with the things she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the quarter, with Faraday's slow careful hand on them; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and a smell of the drowned chapel, which Iska had swum the nave for that morning at low water because Tamsin could not have reached it alone. Iska stood beside her now. She had dried her hair, but she had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nIt took less time than Tamsin had expected. It was the tide book that did it. Pell had it opened at the twenty-fifth of Rimefall and laid beside the register, the green book with the anchor in a ring stamped against the *Gannet*'s inbound entry, and he looked from one to the other several times, and then he looked at Edric Vane.\n\nVane was standing at the side of the hall between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had not slept either, Tamsin thought; he had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane looked at Agathe Sorrel. Everyone in the hall looked at Agathe Sorrel.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" Vane said. \"My lord. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything to say she turned her good eye on him, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row, and said, \"I keep a ledger, my lord. Like any merchant. So does the sea,\" and nothing more, then or afterward.\n\nCaptain Mell was not in the hall. He had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and the justiciar's men had gone after him, and in the end they would bring him back. But that was later. That was another week.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with a week's beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was going out, the great spring ebb that would leave the whole estuary bare to the Teeth before dark, and the light lay gold on the mud. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line a week ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.\n","wordCount":3244,"createdAt":1794284038822},{"id":"F0-FfkvPMUE8MO1Ut1htS","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms, the ledger left behind her wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of the painted St Brannoc's lifted hand where only a swimmer could reach it, and when she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell looked at Corin's coat on its hook. He put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.\n\n# The Assessor's Seal\n\n## Vane's Office\n[\n  Place: #The Exchequer\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Edric Vane @@Wren\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Tamsin's seal:@Edric Vane ^Tide book:@Tamsin Orrey\n  Facts: ?The Gannet could not have crossed the bar on 25 Rimefall:@Edric Vane\n]\n\nShe sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, very precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book — that it was gone. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had told him about the tide books. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #Tidewatch:ruined\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Wren @@Brother Hale:dead\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^Bessaly's letter:destroyed ^The tide-key:@Tamsin Orrey ^Tamsin's seal:@Tamsin Orrey\n]\n\nThe bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a terrible tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Day after tomorrow. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the stars over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"\n","wordCount":2633,"createdAt":1794284019220},{"id":"FFS-H-pqBal9Ou6P66cgB","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer had entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1011,"createdAt":1794287252275},{"id":"FOZyuEt6SINQ8HPl5B7y5","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast across the flats in a long thin silver sheet the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1047,"createdAt":1794286703504},{"id":"Fz220Op2Rw0quffpxedpU","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel.\n\nShe had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made that same day says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. Vane looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, in my office, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she looked at him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with three days' beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line five days ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1359,"createdAt":1794294090955},{"id":"GOFNPqlc2ffuXAFkUhuXE","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":779,"createdAt":1794287229537},{"id":"GP_lfHeMdak2SRBjh24W-","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor. She had lain awake working it out. Vane wanted her quiet, and Mell would do what Vane told him. If she could make Vane afraid enough of what she knew, he might buy her quiet with Corin.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer had entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the fifteenth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I like your Mr Faraday, and he likes you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. Then she looked at Tamsin's throat, where the cord had always been, and her face changed. \"Where's your seal?\"\n\nTamsin told her, in the street, in about four words. Wren listened with her mouth shut tight.\n\n\"Anyway, he won't come,\" she said. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1090,"createdAt":1794294036902},{"id":"HL7t6mgpruQv-qyIJVn16","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1601,"createdAt":1794286699702},{"id":"Hze8epie6Ik1QT6-_YNFX","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew.* She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning. The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1052,"createdAt":1794286731568},{"id":"I3OezUBjjMo9yLa-7t1v7","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet, the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1044,"createdAt":1794287238728},{"id":"ILX5pJpyormmVF1RycBOo","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand nothing, for three years, through the glass door; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1245,"createdAt":1794287262943},{"id":"ISpM4KeFtKAS_gf7Rkc67","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast across the flats in a long thin silver sheet the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1047,"createdAt":1794286702870},{"id":"IUlLNx0vv1m2MoPZkJKW_","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had told him about the tide books. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1018,"createdAt":1794286712368},{"id":"ImgMF5tk70PheY4-SvfLR","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1600,"createdAt":1794286732201},{"id":"KMszunqERRxLr0XEDsHL1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and very short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But Vane knows. Somebody told him I'd been asking about the *Gannet*, and I don't know who, because the only person I asked was him. And then Mother Agathe knew, three hours later. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went very still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.\n\n# Tidewatch\n\n[\n  Place: #Tidewatch\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask @@Brother Hale\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Bessaly's letter:@Brother Hale\n  Facts: ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:@Tamsin Orrey ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:@Corin Dask ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:@Brother Hale ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:reader ?The Gannet could not have crossed the bar on 25 Rimefall:@Tamsin Orrey ?The Gannet could not have crossed the bar on 25 Rimefall:@Corin Dask ?The Gannet could not have crossed the bar on 25 Rimefall:@Brother Hale\n]\n\nThe sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth very slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been asked about it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quite quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line. He looked at it, and then he turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":2771,"createdAt":1794283976237},{"id":"KSLOPUs1SO-YLOmTtB55X","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel. She had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made a week later says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, in my office, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she raised his eyes to him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with a week's beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line a week ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1361,"createdAt":1794287587619},{"id":"KhqVft2x0TnUqn5GtqYzz","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied with tarred twine. \"She went down a month ago, at the start of Rimefall. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the lowest springs. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she had expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest. And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":964,"createdAt":1794287193247},{"id":"Ltvpzf3qJWa0OE2Ls-PY-","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a terrible tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Day after tomorrow. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the stars over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"\n","wordCount":1026,"createdAt":1794284038809},{"id":"N1UT1h9wYc9pxqIf6UyVJ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the last voyage. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor's mark, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1056,"createdAt":1794286688936},{"id":"NAoQCbrmKfqgOmEddoF1L","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1049,"createdAt":1794287197786},{"id":"NIvwQfibaz2kHe149Th6W","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied with tarred twine. \"She went down a month ago, at the start of Rimefall. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the bottom of the ebb, if you know where to look. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she had expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest. And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":972,"createdAt":1794287194777},{"id":"NMDlFAs2T236-nyBNYDJA","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with the Chief Assessor's mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the last voyage. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor's mark, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1057,"createdAt":1794286687670},{"id":"NXqh9KbHaltpaHJb87-ty","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the start of Rimefall, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, in ballast, no duty, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the fifteenth of Rimefall, eighteen days ago, sitting a little close to the line above it, as if it had been written small to fit. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the fifteenth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the fifteenth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1062,"createdAt":1794294029934},{"id":"NasXNdmnKTmsxMLDQFHVv","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out in ballast on the second of Rimefall, bring her round to the Flats and leave her there. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will write the Gannet in again after, laden, as if she had come home, because they must, or there is no cargo to lose. They will put it in among old entries, so it looks old. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The fifteenth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the fifteenth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the fifteenth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the fifteenth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1681,"createdAt":1794294083288},{"id":"NjMLaVTXpbza06Oh9KlHa","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets that went down into the vault each quarter, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand nothing, for three years, through the glass door; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down very slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1203,"createdAt":1794286715537},{"id":"O0a1bVPcV1NoQ12yH3xKP","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out in ballast on the second of Rimefall, bring her round to the Flats and leave her there. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will write the Gannet in again after, laden, as if she had come home, because they must, or there is no cargo to lose. They will put it in among old entries, so it looks old. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The fifteenth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the fifteenth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1681,"createdAt":1794287223468},{"id":"OhyasOkBsshxLvRb4tmiO","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms, the ledger left behind her wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of the painted St Brannoc's lifted hand where only a swimmer could reach it, and when she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell looked at Corin's coat on its hook. He put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.\n","wordCount":522,"createdAt":1794284019209},{"id":"OmKE3IWrvH8Aodm-iAacX","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a terrible tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Day after tomorrow. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the stars over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"","wordCount":1026,"createdAt":1794286713003},{"id":"PW7O8_0twenOZc9_izjfE","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the fortnight she wanted in a minute, *Rimefall 8–21, sealed at the springs of the 22nd*, and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No *Gannet* on the fifteenth. The entries for the fifteenth were all there, the *Marigold* and two Guild barges and a coaster out of Hesk, in Faraday's slow careful hand, and the line that sat so close above them in the green book upstairs was not among them.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copies on the day,\" he said. \"He always has. He's the only man in the building who does.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1298,"createdAt":1794294037534},{"id":"PWWc5yd7LMYg8M_tyeSzO","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the start of Rimefall, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, in ballast, no duty, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the fifteenth of Rimefall, three weeks ago, sitting a little close to the line above it, as if it had been written small to fit. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the fifteenth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the fifteenth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1062,"createdAt":1794294029303},{"id":"PpCNcPbiAF-v6JX5t0-tZ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms, the ledger left behind her wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of the painted St Brannoc's lifted hand where only a swimmer could reach it, and when she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell looked at Corin's coat on its hook. He put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.","wordCount":522,"createdAt":1794286707936},{"id":"Q73JxnruNn1hnSxTr76CJ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1253,"createdAt":1794287264436},{"id":"Rw0zVCPQix4PvYIMsANDp","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew.* She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went very still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1058,"createdAt":1794286695269},{"id":"S28dtfGENP0gnbvHr3RNh","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms, the ledger left behind her wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of the painted St Brannoc's lifted hand where only a swimmer could reach it, and when she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell looked at Corin's coat on its hook. He put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.\n\n# The Assessor's Seal\n\n[\n  Place: #The Exchequer\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Edric Vane @@Wren\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Tamsin's seal:@Edric Vane ^Tide book:@Tamsin Orrey\n  Facts: ?The Gannet could not have crossed the bar on 25 Rimefall:@Edric Vane\n]\n\nShe sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, very precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book — that it was gone. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had told him about the tide books. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1582,"createdAt":1794284019283},{"id":"TBaQ0RfomGBrN5F3jM8Y2","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, and who at seventy-one still sat at the end of the hall copying the Crown's sheets, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied with tarred twine. \"She went down a month ago, at the start of Rimefall. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the bottom of the ebb, if you know where to look. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she had expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest. And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":988,"createdAt":1794294028036},{"id":"TEHiAaBJTM88R9LmY0RUo","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—followed the mudlark up there this afternoon, and sat under the town stair to see who came,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"They came at midnight, the girl and the cripple, round by the sea stair. They were up there an hour. Then they went down the same way.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your assessor. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"And the tower's key. It takes two, Agathe.\"\n\n\"Then Mell's men will bring you the tower's key, when the tower has had its night. Hale wears it round his neck. He won't be needing it.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"The spring tide. Three days: the seventh. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of a scallop shell, to match the lock.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":834,"createdAt":1794287237171},{"id":"TaXr275CTgNVsGAlq2I3x","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms. The ledger was behind her, wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of St Brannoc's lifted hand, where only a swimmer could reach it. When she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell looked at Corin's coat on its hook. He put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.","wordCount":519,"createdAt":1794286709203},{"id":"V4oYhQGN3wABgScT0eYZ8","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"CPvxlKCtqtmDjgg7Nzt7F","text":"It began to rain as she crossed the Saltmarket, a thin cold rain coming in sideways off the water, and by the time she reached Guild Row the hem of her skirt was black to the knee.\n\nSorrel House stood at the top of the Row where the street widened into a little square with a dry fountain in it. It was the oldest of the Guild houses and the only one still built of timber, black oak beams and white plaster, three storeys leaning out over the street so that the upper windows nearly touched the house across the way. People said the Sorrels had refused to rebuild in stone after the Great Flood because stone was for people who meant to leave. Tamsin had always thought that a strange thing to be proud of, but then she had never understood the Guild.\n\nA manservant took her coat and the folder and showed her up to a room on the first floor that smelled of beeswax and peat smoke. The windows were small and leaded and let in almost nothing of the grey afternoon. There was a fire, at least. There was a fire, and in front of it, in a high-backed chair with a rug over her knees, was Mother Agathe Sorrel.\n\nShe was a small woman, much smaller than Tamsin remembered from the Guild processions, and very old. Her hair was white and pulled back so tight it seemed to stretch the skin of her forehead. She wore black, as Guild mothers did, and on every finger of both hands she wore a ring, heavy old rings of gold and jet and green stone that clicked against each other when she moved. Her left eye was clouded milk-white. The right one was dark and quick and missed nothing.\n\n\"The Exchequer's girl,\" she said. \"Sit down. You're dripping on my floor.\"\n\nTamsin sat on the stool she was pointed to. The manservant brought the folder and gave it to Mother Agathe, who did not open it.\n\n\"The quarter's assessment, Mother,\" Tamsin said. \"The Chief Assessor sends his compliments. The Guild's hull duty for the quarter comes to four hundred and twelve crowns, less the rebates already allowed—\"\n\n\"I can read, child. I've been reading Exchequer assessments since before your mother was born.\" Agathe laid the folder on her knees and put one ringed hand flat on top of it. \"What is your name?\"\n\n\"Orrey, Mother. Tamsin Orrey.\"\n\n\"Orrey. From the Quarter.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\"\n\n\"Your father was a lighterman.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Drowned, wasn't he? In the year of the bad ice.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\" Tamsin kept her voice level. She was not sure what was happening, but she was sure that something was.\n\n\"It's a hard trade,\" Agathe said. \"The water takes what it's owed. My husband used to say that the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant, and it always collects.\" The dark eye moved over Tamsin's face, unhurried. \"I hear you've been asking about the *Gannet*.\"\n\nThe fire cracked. Somewhere below them in the house a door closed.\n\n\"I noticed an entry in the register, Mother,\" Tamsin said carefully. \"I mentioned it to the Chief Assessor. It was nothing.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"He said it was nothing.\"\n\n\"Then I expect it was.\" Agathe smiled. Her teeth were small and brown and very even. \"Edric is a sound man. He's been a good friend to this house for twenty years. If he tells you a thing is nothing, child, you may rest your head on it.\" She tapped the folder. \"Four hundred and twelve, you say.\"\n\n\"Less rebates, Mother.\"\n\n\"Less rebates.\" She turned her head toward the door. \"Iska. See Miss Orrey out. And give her something hot before she goes. She's wet through. We don't want the Exchequer saying we send its clerks home to catch their deaths.\"\n\nTamsin had not seen anyone else in the room. But a young woman stood up from the window seat at the far end, where the shadows were deepest, and came forward. She was perhaps twenty-two, tall, with her mother's dark eyes, both of them clear, and a long plain face that might have been handsome if she had not looked so tired. She wore the black of the Guild, but she wore it badly, with ink on the cuffs.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said to Tamsin. \"Kitchen's warmest.\"\n\nThey went down two flights of narrow stairs. Iska Sorrel did not speak on the way, and did not take her to the kitchen. She took her to the side door, where the manservant was waiting with Tamsin's coat, and she took the coat from him and sent him away and held it out herself.\n\n\"You're not getting anything hot,\" she said quietly, \"you're getting out of this house. My mother likes you.\"\n\n\"That's good, isn't it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Iska looked at the door the manservant had gone through, and then back at Tamsin. Up close her face was very young and very serious. \"I'll say this once. Don't go out to the Flats on your own. Whatever you think you've found, don't go and look at it on your own.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"Then good.\" Iska opened the door. The rain was coming down hard now, hammering on the cobbles of the little square, filling the dry fountain. \"Go home, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nTamsin went out into the rain. When she turned at the corner of the square to look back, the side door was shut and there was a light in the window seat on the first floor, and a shape in it, very small and very still.","wordCount":940,"createdAt":1794286692105},{"id":"VO1nDZ7IqZpz4Tq_gjeCL","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1265,"createdAt":1794287267506},{"id":"VgXEL6DaNB4rHMit3l1Og","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea, and in the middle of the nave a stone St Brannoc standing in the water to his chest, with one hand lifted as if to keep something dry. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her. She had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go looking on your own.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed — she couldn't help it — and Iska stared at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Four in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. So she had seen him, Tamsin thought, the shuttered lantern in the shadow of the wall; she had seen him and told herself she had not. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":931,"createdAt":1794287247824},{"id":"WIfDsLMCqcqdEhMvDhtkh","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the fortnight she wanted in a minute, *Rimefall 8–21, sealed at the springs of the 22nd*, and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No *Gannet* on the fifteenth. The entries for the fifteenth were all there, the *Marigold* and two Guild barges and a coaster out of Hesk, in Faraday's slow careful hand, and the line that sat so close above them in the green book upstairs was not among them.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1294,"createdAt":1794287272098},{"id":"WMjvhaWbh4nVW6dglC5Y1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the start of Rimefall, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, in ballast, no duty, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1044,"createdAt":1794287200793},{"id":"WwMKwtYRPuXkz-vSH0iJl","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out in ballast on the second of Rimefall, bring her round to the Flats and leave her there. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will write the Gannet in again after, laden, as if she had come home, because they must, or there is no cargo to lose. They will put it in among old entries, so it looks old. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The fifteenth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1681,"createdAt":1794287221937},{"id":"XN0a88wJ2t0rfkgR4UAa4","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, and who at seventy-one still sat at the end of the Ledger Hall copying the Crown's sheets, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied with tarred twine. \"She went down a month ago, at the start of Rimefall. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the bottom of the ebb, if you know where to look. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she had expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest. And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":989,"createdAt":1794294080209},{"id":"XQgA_-aFz9RaAat8DpkWs","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a pistol shot, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was quite calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska didn't catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.\n\n# Breme Flats\n\n## The Salt Pans\n[\n  Place: #Cray's End\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Kittiwake bell:@Corin Dask\n]\n\n\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast across the flats in a long thin silver sheet the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.\n\n[\n  Place: #St Brannoc's\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Iska Sorrel\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The second book:@Tamsin Orrey\n  Facts: ?Agathe means to burn Tidewatch:@Tamsin Orrey\n  Relationships: ~Agathe Sorrel/Iska Sorrel:Estranged\n]\n\nIska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc's that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray. Tamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her, and she had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed, she couldn't help it, and Iska looked at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":2756,"createdAt":1794283995817},{"id":"XeAgMimjHJQ8OhX7nnIAL","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor. She had lain awake working it out. Vane wanted her quiet, and Mell would do what Vane told him. If she could make Vane afraid enough of what she knew, he might buy her quiet with Corin.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer had entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I like your Mr Faraday, and he likes you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. Then she looked at Tamsin's throat, where the cord had always been, and her face changed. \"Where's your seal?\"\n\nTamsin told her, in the street, in about four words. Wren listened with her mouth shut tight.\n\n\"Anyway, he won't come,\" she said. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1090,"createdAt":1794287273583},{"id":"Xyg4NBRi2foUqr9HdYeGR","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"CPvxlKCtqtmDjgg7Nzt7F","text":"It began to rain as she crossed the Saltmarket, a thin cold rain coming in sideways off the water, and by the time she reached Guild Row the hem of her skirt was black to the knee.\n\nSorrel House stood at the top of the Row where the street widened into a little square with a dry fountain in it. It was the oldest of the Guild houses and the only one still built of timber, black oak beams and white plaster, three storeys leaning out over the street so that the upper windows nearly touched the house across the way. People said the Sorrels had refused to rebuild in stone after the Great Flood because stone was for people who meant to leave. Tamsin had always thought that a strange thing to be proud of, but then she had never understood the Guild.\n\nA manservant took her coat and the folder and showed her up to a room on the first floor that smelled of beeswax and peat smoke. The windows were small and leaded and let in almost nothing of the grey afternoon. There was a fire, at least. There was a fire, and in front of it, in a high-backed chair with a rug over her knees, was Mother Agathe Sorrel.\n\nShe was a small woman, much smaller than Tamsin remembered from the Guild processions, and very old. Her hair was white and pulled back so tight it seemed to stretch the skin of her forehead. She wore black, as Guild mothers did, and on every finger of both hands she wore a ring, heavy old rings of gold and jet and green stone that clicked against each other when she moved. Her left eye was clouded milk-white. The right one was dark and quick and missed nothing.\n\n\"The Exchequer's girl,\" she said. \"Sit down. You're dripping on my floor.\"\n\nTamsin sat on the stool she was pointed to. The manservant brought the folder and gave it to Mother Agathe, who did not open it.\n\n\"The quarter's assessment, Mother,\" Tamsin said. \"The Chief Assessor sends his compliments. The Guild's hull duty for the quarter comes to four hundred and twelve crowns, less the rebates already allowed—\"\n\n\"I can read, child. I've been reading Exchequer assessments since before your mother was born.\" Agathe laid the folder on her knees and put one ringed hand flat on top of it. \"What is your name?\"\n\n\"Orrey, Mother. Tamsin Orrey.\"\n\n\"Orrey. From the Quarter.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\"\n\n\"Your father was a lighterman.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Drowned, wasn't he? In the year of the bad ice.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\" Tamsin kept her voice level. She was not sure what was happening, but she was sure that something was.\n\n\"It's a hard trade,\" Agathe said. \"The water takes what it's owed. My husband used to say that the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant, and it always collects.\" The dark eye moved over Tamsin's face, unhurried. \"I hear you've been asking about the *Gannet*.\"\n\nThe fire cracked. Somewhere below them in the house a door closed.\n\n\"I noticed an entry in the register, Mother,\" Tamsin said carefully. \"I mentioned it to the Chief Assessor. It was nothing.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"He said it was nothing.\"\n\n\"Then I expect it was.\" Agathe smiled. Her teeth were small and brown and very even. \"Edric is a sound man. He's been a good friend to this house for twenty years. If he tells you a thing is nothing, child, you may rest your head on it.\" She tapped the folder. \"Four hundred and twelve, you say.\"\n\n\"Less rebates, Mother.\"\n\n\"Less rebates.\" She turned her head toward the door. \"Iska. See Miss Orrey out. And give her something hot before she goes. She's wet through. We don't want the Exchequer saying we send its clerks home to catch their deaths.\"\n\nTamsin had not seen anyone else in the room. But a young woman stood up from the window seat at the far end, where the shadows were deepest, and came forward. She was perhaps twenty-two, tall, with her mother's dark eyes, both of them clear, and a long plain face that might have been handsome if she had not looked so tired. She wore the black of the Guild, but she wore it badly, with ink on the cuffs.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said to Tamsin. \"Kitchen's warmest.\"\n\nThey went down two flights of narrow stairs. Iska Sorrel did not speak on the way, and did not take her to the kitchen. She took her to the side door, where the manservant was waiting with Tamsin's coat, and she took the coat from him and sent him away and held it out herself.\n\n\"You're not getting anything hot,\" she said quietly. \"You're getting out of this house. My mother likes you.\"\n\n\"That's good, isn't it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Iska glanced at the door the manservant had gone through, and then back at Tamsin. Up close her face was very young and very serious. \"I'll say this once. Don't go out to the Flats on your own. Whatever you think you've found, don't go and look at it on your own.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"Then good.\" Iska opened the door. The rain was coming down hard now, hammering on the cobbles of the little square, filling the dry fountain. \"Go home, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nTamsin went out into the rain. When she turned at the corner of the square to look back, the side door was shut and there was a light in the window seat on the first floor, and a shape in it, very small and very still.","wordCount":940,"createdAt":1794287205369},{"id":"Y5wTULrUFSlxVLIsPNMe7","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"CPvxlKCtqtmDjgg7Nzt7F","text":"It began to rain as she crossed the Saltmarket, a thin cold rain coming in sideways off the water, and by the time she reached Guild Row the hem of her skirt was black to the knee.\n\nSorrel House stood at the top of the Row where the street widened into a little square with a dry fountain in it. It was the oldest of the Guild houses and the only one still built of timber, black oak beams and white plaster, three storeys leaning out over the street so that the upper windows nearly touched the house across the way. People said the Sorrels had refused to rebuild in stone after the Great Flood because stone was for people who meant to leave. Tamsin had always thought that a strange thing to be proud of, but then she had never understood the Guild.\n\nA manservant took her coat and the folder and showed her up to a room on the first floor that smelled of beeswax and peat smoke. The windows were small and leaded and let in almost nothing of the grey afternoon. There was a fire, at least. There was a fire, and in front of it, in a high-backed chair with a rug over her knees, was Mother Agathe Sorrel.\n\nShe was a small woman, much smaller than Tamsin remembered from the Guild processions, and very old. Her hair was white and pulled back so tight it seemed to stretch the skin of her forehead. She wore black, as Guild mothers did, and on every finger of both hands she wore a ring, heavy old rings of gold and jet and green stone that clicked against each other when she moved. Her left eye was clouded milk-white. The right one was dark and quick and missed nothing.\n\n\"The Exchequer's girl,\" she said. \"Sit down. You're dripping on my floor.\"\n\nTamsin sat on the stool she was pointed to. The manservant brought the folder and gave it to Mother Agathe, who did not open it.\n\n\"The quarter's assessment, Mother,\" Tamsin said. \"The Chief Assessor sends his compliments. The Guild's hull duty for the quarter comes to four hundred and twelve crowns, less the rebates already allowed—\"\n\n\"I can read, child. I've been reading Exchequer assessments since before your mother was born.\" Agathe laid the folder on her knees and put one ringed hand flat on top of it. \"What is your name?\"\n\n\"Orrey, Mother. Tamsin Orrey.\"\n\n\"Orrey. From the Quarter.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\"\n\n\"Your father was a lighterman.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Drowned, wasn't he? In the year of the bad ice.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\" Tamsin kept her voice level. She was not sure what was happening, but she was sure that something was.\n\n\"It's a hard trade,\" Agathe said. \"The water takes what it's owed. My husband used to say that the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant, and it always collects.\" The dark eye moved over Tamsin's face, unhurried. \"I hear you've been asking about the *Gannet*.\"\n\nThe fire cracked. Somewhere below them in the house a door closed.\n\n\"I noticed an entry in the register, Mother,\" Tamsin said carefully. \"I mentioned it to the Chief Assessor. It was nothing.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"He said it was nothing.\"\n\n\"Then I expect it was.\" Agathe smiled. Her teeth were small and brown and very even. \"Edric is a sound man. He's been a good friend to this house for twenty years. If he tells you a thing is nothing, child, you may rest your head on it.\" She tapped the folder. \"Four hundred and twelve, you say.\"\n\n\"Less rebates, Mother.\"\n\n\"Less rebates.\" She turned her head toward the door. \"Iska. See Miss Orrey out. And give her something hot before she goes. She's wet through. We don't want the Exchequer saying we send its clerks home to catch their deaths.\"\n\nTamsin had not seen anyone else in the room. But a young woman stood up from the window seat at the far end, where the shadows were deepest, and came forward. She was perhaps twenty-two, tall, with her mother's dark eyes, both of them clear, and a long plain face that might have been handsome if she had not looked so tired. She wore the black of the Guild, but she wore it badly, with ink on the cuffs.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said to Tamsin. \"Kitchen's warmest.\"\n\nThey went down two flights of narrow stairs. Iska Sorrel did not speak on the way, and did not take her to the kitchen. She took her to the side door, where the manservant was waiting with Tamsin's coat, and she took the coat from him and sent him away and held it out herself.\n\n\"You're not getting anything hot,\" she said quietly. \"You're getting out of this house. My mother likes you.\"\n\n\"That's good, isn't it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Iska glanced at the door the manservant had gone through, and then back at Tamsin. Up close her face was very young and very serious. \"I'll say this once. Whatever you think you've found, don't go looking for it on your own. Not anywhere.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"Then good.\" Iska opened the door. The rain was coming down hard now, hammering on the cobbles of the little square, filling the dry fountain. \"Go home, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nTamsin went out into the rain. When she turned at the corner of the square to look back, the side door was shut and there was a light in the window seat on the first floor, and a shape in it, very small and very still.","wordCount":932,"createdAt":1794287206931},{"id":"YJSdVCngG6jtjvTHNF4cj","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Wanted the key. Couldn't — find it. Then oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Tomorrow. At dawn. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the stars over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"","wordCount":1033,"createdAt":1794294084853},{"id":"Z3uKpeZeB9wW3vztaO4eR","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her. She had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed — she couldn't help it — and Iska looked at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":876,"createdAt":1794286707302},{"id":"ZSJUuZ0_JiYqetXncfKqA","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1716,"createdAt":1794287215987},{"id":"ZZ21UPYNnh0BK43g4vEKR","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew, three hours later. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went very still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1061,"createdAt":1794286694636},{"id":"_02oxBWV2oZIjUXIOY8ij","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew.* She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning. The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1053,"createdAt":1794286696536},{"id":"_1zDFFDgpZ93T-B9XHY2T","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel. She had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made a week later says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, in my office, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she looked at him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with three days' beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line five days ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1359,"createdAt":1794294039434},{"id":"aQmg_tKvB1F6nl7sZm8q_","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the start of Rimefall, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, in ballast, no duty, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the fifteenth of Rimefall, three weeks ago, sitting a little close to the line above it, as if it had been written small to fit. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1062,"createdAt":1794287202319},{"id":"c9gNTZZIFMA8ZuvNSemaF","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets in old Faraday's hand that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the fortnight she wanted in a minute, *Rimefall 8–21, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second*, and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No *Gannet* on the fifteenth. The entries for the fifteenth were all there, the *Marigold* and two Guild barges and a coaster out of Hesk, in Faraday's slow careful hand, and the line that sat so close above them in the green book upstairs was not among them.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copies on the day,\" he said. \"He always has. He's the only man in the building who does.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1295,"createdAt":1794294038802},{"id":"doihgn9ADsgu8bsa41pzn","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the front, at the long table where the witnesses stood, with the things she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the quarter, with Faraday's slow careful hand on them; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and a smell of the drowned chapel, which Iska had swum the nave for that morning at low water because Tamsin could not have reached it alone. Iska stood beside her now. She had dried her hair, but she had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nIt took less time than Tamsin had expected. It was the tide book that did it. Pell had it opened at the twenty-fifth of Rimefall and laid beside the register, the green book with the anchor in a ring stamped against the *Gannet*'s inbound entry, and he looked from one to the other several times, and then he looked at Edric Vane.\n\nVane was standing at the side of the hall between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had not slept either, Tamsin thought; he had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane looked at Agathe Sorrel. Everyone in the hall looked at Agathe Sorrel.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" Vane said. \"My lord. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything to say she turned her good eye on him, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row, and said, \"I keep a ledger, my lord. Like any merchant. So does the sea,\" and nothing more, then or afterward.\n\nCaptain Mell was not in the hall. He had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and the justiciar's men had gone after him, and in the end they would bring him back. But that was later. That was another week.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with a week's beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was going out, the great spring ebb that would leave the whole estuary bare to the Teeth before dark, and the light lay gold on the mud. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line a week ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.\n","wordCount":954,"createdAt":1794287392949},{"id":"f8q-npV23wORkK2C-voCH","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1049,"createdAt":1794287196260},{"id":"f_5Cp5220i5-y1zD2oXMk","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a pistol shot, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska didn't catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":779,"createdAt":1794286701604},{"id":"fb_K9KsB4gQIJv9lBNDE9","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea, and in the middle of the nave a stone St Brannoc standing in the water to his chest, with one hand lifted as if to keep something dry. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her. She had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go looking on your own.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed — she couldn't help it — and Iska stared at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Four in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":905,"createdAt":1794287246267},{"id":"fqBBL8DL-LirCpm6hx6ni","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a pistol shot, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":780,"createdAt":1794287226530},{"id":"gDEm8qVX06-WX4aXxQdNy","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"CPvxlKCtqtmDjgg7Nzt7F","text":"It began to rain as she crossed the Saltmarket, a thin cold rain coming in sideways off the water, and by the time she reached Guild Row the hem of her skirt was black to the knee.\n\nSorrel House stood at the top of the Row where the street widened into a little square with a dry fountain in it. It was the oldest of the Guild houses and the only one still built of timber, black oak beams and white plaster, three storeys leaning out over the street so that the upper windows nearly touched the house across the way. People said the Sorrels had refused to rebuild in stone after the Great Flood because stone was for people who meant to leave. Tamsin had always thought that a strange thing to be proud of, but then she had never understood the Guild.\n\nA manservant took her coat and the folder and showed her up to a room on the first floor that smelled of beeswax and peat smoke. The windows were small and leaded and let in almost nothing of the grey afternoon. There was a fire, at least. There was a fire, and in front of it, in a high-backed chair with a rug over her knees, was Mother Agathe Sorrel.\n\nShe was a small woman, much smaller than Tamsin remembered from the Guild processions, and very old. Her hair was white and pulled back so tight it seemed to stretch the skin of her forehead. She wore black, as Guild mothers did, and on every finger of both hands she wore a ring, heavy old rings of gold and jet and green stone that clicked against each other when she moved. Her left eye was clouded milk-white. The right one was dark and quick and missed nothing.\n\n\"The Exchequer's girl,\" she said. \"Sit down. You're dripping on my floor.\"\n\nTamsin sat on the stool she was pointed to. The manservant brought the folder and gave it to Mother Agathe, who did not open it.\n\n\"The quarter's assessment, Mother,\" Tamsin said. \"The Chief Assessor sends his compliments. The Guild's hull duty for the quarter comes to four hundred and twelve crowns, less the rebates already allowed—\"\n\n\"I can read, child. I've been reading Exchequer assessments since before your mother was born.\" Agathe laid the folder on her knees and put one ringed hand flat on top of it. \"What is your name?\"\n\n\"Orrey, Mother. Tamsin Orrey.\"\n\n\"Orrey. From the Quarter.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\"\n\n\"Your father was a lighterman.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Drowned, wasn't he? In the year of the bad ice.\"\n\n\"Yes, Mother.\" Tamsin kept her voice level. She was not sure what was happening, but she was sure that something was.\n\n\"It's a hard trade,\" Agathe said. \"The water takes what it's owed. My husband used to say that the sea keeps a ledger like any other merchant, and it always collects.\" The dark eye moved over Tamsin's face, unhurried. \"I hear you've been asking about the *Gannet*.\"\n\nThe fire cracked. Somewhere below them in the house a door closed.\n\n\"I noticed an entry in the register, Mother,\" Tamsin said carefully. \"I mentioned it to the Chief Assessor. It was nothing.\"\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"He said it was nothing.\"\n\n\"Then I expect it was.\" Agathe smiled. Her teeth were small and brown and very even. \"Edric is a sound man. He's been a good friend to this house for twenty years. If he tells you a thing is nothing, child, you may rest your head on it.\" She tapped the folder. \"Four hundred and twelve, you say.\"\n\n\"Less rebates, Mother.\"\n\n\"Less rebates.\" She turned her head toward the door. \"Iska. See Miss Orrey out. And give her something hot before she goes, she's wet through. We don't want the Exchequer saying we send its clerks home to catch their deaths.\"\n\nTamsin had not seen anyone else in the room. But a young woman stood up from the window seat at the far end, where the shadows were deepest, and came forward. She was perhaps twenty-two, tall, with her mother's dark eyes, both of them clear, and a long plain face that might have been handsome if she had not looked so tired. She wore the black of the Guild, but she wore it badly, with ink on the cuffs.\n\n\"Come on,\" she said to Tamsin. \"Kitchen's warmest.\"\n\nThey went down two flights of narrow stairs. Iska Sorrel did not speak on the way, and did not take her to the kitchen. She took her to the side door, where the manservant was waiting with Tamsin's coat, and she took the coat from him and sent him away and held it out herself.\n\n\"You're not getting anything hot,\" she said quietly, \"you're getting out of this house. My mother likes you.\"\n\n\"That's good, isn't it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Iska looked at the door the manservant had gone through, and then back at Tamsin. Up close her face was very young and very serious. \"I'll say this once. Don't go out to the Flats on your own. Whatever you think you've found, don't go and look at it on your own.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"Then good.\" Iska opened the door. The rain was coming down hard now, hammering on the cobbles of the little square, filling the dry fountain. \"Go home, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nTamsin went out into the rain. When she turned at the corner of the square to look back, the side door was shut and there was a light in the window seat on the first floor, and a shape in it, very small and very still.","wordCount":940,"createdAt":1794286691471},{"id":"gMGKkaDvu965kmKDyi0wJ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms. The ledger was behind her, wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of St Brannoc's lifted hand, where only a swimmer could reach it. When she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell crossed to Corin's coat on its hook and put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.","wordCount":519,"createdAt":1794287249290},{"id":"gMKxD9ui5vxNSGqnavS0q","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a pistol shot, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was quite calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska didn't catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.\n\n# Breme Flats\n\n## The Salt Pans\n[\n  Place: #Cray's End\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Kittiwake bell:@Corin Dask\n]\n\n\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast across the flats in a long thin silver sheet the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.\n\n## The Drowned Quarter\n[\n  Place: #St Brannoc's\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Iska Sorrel\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The second book:@Tamsin Orrey\n  Facts: ?Agathe means to burn Tidewatch:@Tamsin Orrey\n  Relationships: ~Agathe Sorrel/Iska Sorrel:Estranged\n]\n\nIska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc's that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray. Tamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her, and she had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed, she couldn't help it, and Iska looked at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Patience\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask @@Wren @@Oszkar Mell\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Kittiwake bell:@Oszkar Mell ^The second book:#St Brannoc's\n]\n\nThey came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms, the ledger left behind her wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of the painted St Brannoc's lifted hand where only a swimmer could reach it, and when she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell looked at Corin's coat on its hook. He put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.\n","wordCount":3307,"createdAt":1794283995773},{"id":"gQjTgGNKO_w9v6o8NHmAg","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with the Chief Assessor's mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the last voyage. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor's mark, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.\n","wordCount":1057,"createdAt":1794283945112},{"id":"gSXWGXnqgHVk91AIF9AtP","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1010,"createdAt":1794286732834},{"id":"gYELC70MpaKhFrNpE7IVT","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a pistol shot, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was quite calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska didn't catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":780,"createdAt":1794286700338},{"id":"i3T5kMO5dexD1_J-dnBIc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out in ballast on the second of Rimefall, bring her round to the Flats and leave her there. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will write the Gannet in again after, laden, as if she had come home, because they must, or there is no cargo to lose. They will put it in among old entries, so it looks old. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1681,"createdAt":1794287220439},{"id":"iBcHm8vJiq9mlBbm0PiD6","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and very short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But Vane knows. Somebody told him I'd been asking about the *Gannet*, and I don't know who, because the only person I asked was him. And then Mother Agathe knew, three hours later. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went very still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.\n\n# Tidewatch\n\n## The Lamp\n[\n  Place: #Tidewatch\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask @@Brother Hale\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Bessaly's letter:@Brother Hale\n  Facts: ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:@Tamsin Orrey ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:@Corin Dask ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:@Brother Hale ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:reader ?The Gannet could not have crossed the bar on 25 Rimefall:@Tamsin Orrey ?The Gannet could not have crossed the bar on 25 Rimefall:@Corin Dask ?The Gannet could not have crossed the bar on 25 Rimefall:@Brother Hale\n]\n\nThe sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth very slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been asked about it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quite quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line. He looked at it, and then he turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.\n\n[\n  Place: #Sorrel House\n  Characters: @@Iska Sorrel @@Agathe Sorrel @@Edric Vane\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The second book:@Iska Sorrel\n  Facts: ?Agathe means to burn Tidewatch:@Iska Sorrel ?Agathe means to burn Tidewatch:reader ?The Guild sells ships to the sea:@Iska Sorrel\n]\n\nIska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a pistol shot, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was quite calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska didn't catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.\n","wordCount":3594,"createdAt":1794283976159},{"id":"iyOCQoBGCuCaSXT6ICjbt","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray. Tamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her, and she had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed, she couldn't help it, and Iska looked at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":875,"createdAt":1794286705402},{"id":"iz7dEEewH1gd7jgig_ESx","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been asked about it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quite quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line. He looked at it, and then he turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1608,"createdAt":1794286697802},{"id":"jBlh3g-UaXyPc3lwMeih5","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt was the morning of the fourth, she had slept three hours, and the Ledger Hall had expected her at eight; she had decided on the walk down to the Quarter not to go, and had not let herself think about what that would cost. It had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She stopped there too, with the lantern held close. The *Constance* had been on the mud that morning, her name freshly gilded. One letter, and a coat of paint. She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet, the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1118,"createdAt":1794294033100},{"id":"jCFgKBbO0AsxycuA39lkf","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth very slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been asked about it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quite quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line. He looked at it, and then he turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1609,"createdAt":1794286697171},{"id":"kfuiuxC5581CINGdjITKk","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Wanted the key. Couldn't — find it. Then oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Tomorrow. At dawn. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the moon over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"","wordCount":1033,"createdAt":1794294086396},{"id":"ksDKzTbY81QeJgHFKUcCH","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But Vane knows. Somebody told him I'd been asking about the *Gannet*, and I don't know who, because the only person I asked was him. And then Mother Agathe knew, three hours later. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went very still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1073,"createdAt":1794286694003},{"id":"kxfz37ehHhMHDYTHT-cIq","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"f3ouhrOBfX6Ca5ORW63oh","text":"The bell of the Saltmarket began to ring at a little before two in the morning, and Tamsin was already awake.\n\nShe had not undressed. She had sat up all evening in her rooms on Lantern Stair, with the tide book on the table in front of her and Wren asleep in her bed, listening to the town go quiet. She had gone to the window a hundred times. From her window you could see, over the roofs, the top of Tidewatch and its slow sweeping light, and every time she looked the light was there, turning, bright and dim, bright and dim, and she had begun to think that perhaps it would be all right, that perhaps Agathe would wait after all, that perhaps Iska had been wrong.\n\nThen she looked, and the light was the wrong colour. Orange, not white. And it was not turning.\n\nShe was down the stairs and in the street before the bell started. Wren was behind her; she had not woken Wren, but Wren was there, barefoot, running. The streets were full of people coming out of doors in their nightclothes, pointing. Everyone in Quell knew what the light meant. If the light at Tidewatch went out there would be ships on the Teeth by morning.\n\nThey ran down through the Saltmarket and along the harbour front to the foot of the tower. A crowd had gathered on the quay at the bottom of the town stair, and the watch were there, holding them back, and above them the top of the tower was burning. Fire came out of the lamp-room windows in sheets. Glass was falling, bright, tinkling on the stones.\n\n\"Is he out?\" Tamsin was shouting at the nearest watchman. \"The brother, is he out, has anyone gone up—\"\n\n\"Stand back, miss. Nobody's going up there.\"\n\nShe did not stand back. She went round the foot of the tower to the sea side, where there was no crowd, and up the sea stair with the rusted rail, and Wren came after her and she did not have breath to send her back.\n\nShe had climbed it two nights ago with Corin calling out the gaps. She remembered them now: the seventeenth step, the fifty-second, the long stretch above the ninetieth where the rail was gone altogether and the wind came straight off the sea. The smoke was coming down the stair from above, thick and choking, and she pulled her shawl over her mouth and kept her other hand on the wall and climbed.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside it was all smoke and roaring and a terrible light. The great lens had cracked across and fallen from its stand. The shelves along the far wall were burning, forty years of tide books burning, the gold on their spines running in little bright streams. And on the floor by the table, half under it, as if he had tried to get under it to the air, was Brother Hale.\n\nShe got her hands under his arms and pulled. He was so light. He was so light it was like pulling a coat. She got him out onto the stair, into the wind, and the smoke rolled out over them and away, and she knelt on the step with his head in her lap.\n\nHe was alive. His face was black with soot and his eyebrows were gone and one of his hands was burned so badly she could not look at it. But his eyes opened, grey and clear, and found her.\n\n\"Exchequer,\" he said.\n\n\"Don't talk.\"\n\n\"Must.\" He coughed, a terrible tearing cough. \"Mell. Two men. Came up the town stair. Oil.\" He coughed again. \"I should have — gone down. You said. I'm a stubborn — old—\"\n\n\"Brother, please, they're coming, they'll get you down—\"\n\n\"Listen.\" His good hand moved at his chest, under the robe. She helped him, and found a cord round his neck, and on it a key: brass, long and heavy, its bow worked in the shape of a wave. \"Flood vault. Under the Exchequer. Two keys. Chief Assessor has one. Tower has the other. Old law. So that no — one man — can—\" He could not finish. He pushed the key at her. \"Spring tide. Day after tomorrow. The water lifts the weight. Then the door will open.\"\n\nShe took it. It was warm from him.\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Ruth's letter. Is it—\"\n\nHe looked past her, at the doorway, at the fire.\n\n\"Burned,\" he said. \"I'm sorry. Kept it where it was. Stubborn.\" His eyes came back to her. \"You have the book. The tide book. That's what — matters. Numbers don't — lie. Ruth knew.\" He smiled, or tried to. \"Lazy. Never looked at the tide.\"\n\nThen his eyes went past her again, up, at the stars over the sea, and stayed there.\n\nShe did not know how long she knelt there. A long time. The watch came up the sea stair eventually, with buckets and a ladder, and somebody took him out of her lap, and somebody else put a blanket round her, and she let them. On the quay below, the crowd was quiet now. The fire in the lamp-room had burned down to a red glow. Out on the water there was no light at all, and she thought of the Teeth in the dark, and the ships that would be out there tonight not knowing.\n\nWren was sitting on the step below her with her knees drawn up. When the watch had gone down with the brother, she reached into the front of her shirt and took something out and held it up.\n\nIt was a small brass seal on a cord, with a number cut in it. Two hundred and six.\n\n\"Hobb lets me run messages up to the Ledger Hall,\" Wren said. \"The glass man always leaves his drawer unlocked when he goes out for his dinner.\" She put the seal into Tamsin's hand and closed Tamsin's fingers over it, the way Tamsin had once closed Wren's fingers over a copper. \"I thought you'd want it back.\"\n\n# Spring Tide\n\n[\n  Place: #The flood vault\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Edric Vane\n  When: +1 day\n  Facts: ?Iska has the second book:@Edric Vane\n]\n\nThe spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, for the same reason and in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets that went down into the vault each quarter, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand nothing, for three years, through the glass door; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said.\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down very slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":2258,"createdAt":1794284038896},{"id":"lINzg-4d9B0cjFNPV7byO","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—followed the mudlark up there this afternoon, and sat under the town stair to see who came,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"They came at midnight, the girl and the cripple, round by the sea stair. They were up there an hour. Then they went down the same way.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your assessor. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":791,"createdAt":1794287232631},{"id":"lXybypFJ2Q1dtPLslsY79","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line. He looked at it, and then he turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1607,"createdAt":1794286699069},{"id":"lkRItP5xs0Z9XDG63NlhP","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She stopped there too, with the lantern held close. The *Constance* had been on the mud that morning, her name freshly gilded. One letter, and a coat of paint. She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet, the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1073,"createdAt":1794287240235},{"id":"m4EmYH8svm1pv5BtGBvc1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. And if your stamp on the copies says what the register says, then it's your word against the tide book, and you might come out of it. But if it doesn't — if somebody along the way didn't bother copying a lie onto a sheet that was going to sit in the dark for a year—\" She took a breath. \"Then it's only her, isn't it. You can say she made you. You can say it to the justiciar.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their quarters written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1259,"createdAt":1794287265935},{"id":"mVj-WFoczwmACu6RwWd-S","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and very short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But Vane knows. Somebody told him I'd been asking about the *Gannet*, and I don't know who, because the only person I asked was him. And then Mother Agathe knew, three hours later. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went very still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1074,"createdAt":1794286693372},{"id":"mWti97nKieNtbsgi-9twV","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt was the morning of the fourth. She had slept three hours, and the Ledger Hall had expected her at eight. She had decided on the walk down to the Quarter not to go, and had not let herself think about what that would cost. It had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She stopped there too, and brought the lantern close. The *Constance* had been on the mud the morning before, her name freshly gilded. One letter, and a coat of paint. She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet, the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1119,"createdAt":1794294035633},{"id":"nEkRtXsG13O8mFdTzn74E","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms. The ledger was behind her, wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of St Brannoc's lifted hand, where only a swimmer could reach it. When she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell looked at Corin's coat on its hook. He put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.","wordCount":519,"createdAt":1794286708572},{"id":"nJBK9IB-6lDOw93T4kTDm","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe considered her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. She set it down for him as plainly as a manifest, in four lines. First a ship was entered outbound in ballast, empty, with nothing to pay. Then she was taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere she was not known. Then, a week or two later, she was written in again, inbound and laden, forty tons of salt in her hold and the duty paid, as though she had come home from the north coast in the ordinary way. And last she was entered outbound once more, still laden, and lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale, lost with all hands — and the Guild claimed the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever it cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth, and the cargo had never been there.\n\nThe inbound entry was the whole trick. Without it a lost ship was a lost empty ship, and the Crown paid nothing for an empty hold. With it, the Crown paid for forty tons of salt that had only ever existed in the green book on the lectern. The real salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out in ballast on the second of Rimefall, bring her round to the Flats and leave her there. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line, and turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt-barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1680,"createdAt":1794287218940},{"id":"nQAkq1vtsUYJL-R9sIUrC","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—followed the mudlark up there this evening, and sat under the town stair to see who came,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"They came at midnight, the girl and the cripple, round by the sea stair. They were up there an hour. Then they went down the same way.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your assessor. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"And the tower's key. It takes two, Agathe.\"\n\n\"Then Mell's men will bring you the tower's key, when the tower has had its night. Hale wears it round his neck. He won't be needing it.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"The spring tide. Three days: the seventh. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of a scallop shell, to match the lock.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck four. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":834,"createdAt":1794294032467},{"id":"nzQ4CDD6ok_e_5jOO4T2g","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel.\n\nShe had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made that same day says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, in my office, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she looked at him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with three days' beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line five days ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1359,"createdAt":1794294040701},{"id":"oVdU-HzP8toqJ_Hq6Nkux","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets in old Faraday's hand that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the fortnight she wanted in a minute, *Rimefall 8–21, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second*, and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\nNo *Gannet* on the fifteenth. The entries for the fifteenth were all there, the *Marigold* and two Guild barges and a coaster out of Hesk, in Faraday's slow careful hand, and the line that sat so close above them in the green book upstairs was not among them.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copies on the day,\" he said. \"He always has. He's the only man in the building who does.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1283,"createdAt":1794294087895},{"id":"ocCMEt66z8kyXC9QGEBTN","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her, and she had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed, she couldn't help it, and Iska looked at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":875,"createdAt":1794286706037},{"id":"pG1vXkSsVO4Odhe7peWgA","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"1jMiymPcwarg1wBugtTjR","text":"The Tide Court sat at noon the next day, the eighth of Thawmonth, in the old hall under Saltmarket Cross, which had been a fish market before it was a court and still smelled faintly of it in wet weather. It was not wet weather. It was the first bright day since autumn, a hard blue sky and a wind off the sea that sent the gulls sideways and rattled every shutter in the town, and the hall was full to the doors.\n\nTamsin had not known so many people would come. She stood at the witnesses' table with what she had brought laid out in front of her: the Crown's copy sheets for the fortnight, sealed at the springs of the twenty-second and broken by her own thumb; a brown calf tide book with the year stamped on its spine in gold; and the ledger, the second book, with the sacking still on it and the smell of the drowned chapel. She had swum the nave for it herself at the noon low water the day before, with Iska holding the lantern on the gallery, and the stone saint had given it up as if he had been waiting to be asked. Iska stood beside her now. She had not changed out of her Guild black, and she held herself very straight.\n\nJusticiar Pell sat on the bench under the Margravine's arms. He was a thin, dry, middle-aged man who had come down from Hesk the year before and was said to dislike Quell, its smell, its tides and its Guild, in roughly that order. He had read the ledger already. He had sat with it for two hours that morning in the robing room, and he had come out looking like a man who had bitten something bad.\n\nMother Agathe Sorrel sat at the other table in her high-backed chair, which four Guild men had carried down from Sorrel House, with a rug over her knees and a lawyer from Hesk at her elbow whom she had not once let speak. She did not look at her daughter.\n\nTamsin had expected it to be quick. It was not.\n\n\"My lord,\" said Agathe, when Pell asked her to answer, \"you have in front of you a book in a hand like mine, brought to you by a girl who ran from my house in the night, and who has wanted to be anything but my daughter since she was twelve years old. You have some loose sheets from the Exchequer cellar, copied by an old man who is half blind, which do not say a thing that the register does say. And you have the tide book of a priest who, God rest him, set his own tower on fire with his own lamp. That is what you have.\" She turned her good eye on the room, slowly, the way she had turned it on Tamsin in the room above Guild Row. \"Sorrel House has carried salt for the Crown for three hundred years. In that time the sea has taken sixty-one of our ships. I can name them. I keep a ledger, my lord, like any merchant. Ask me which of my ships went down and I will tell you, and I will tell you who drowned.\"\n\nIt was very good. Tamsin watched it work on the hall, and then on Pell, who sat back and put his fingertips together and looked at the second book as though it might, after all, have been written by anyone.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" he said. \"The copy sheets. A copy can be careless.\"\n\n\"It can, my lord.\" Her voice came out smaller than she wanted, and she made it bigger. \"So I'll not ask you to trust the copy. Ask the tide.\" She opened the brown calf book at the fifteenth of Rimefall and laid it beside the green register, where the *Gannet* came home laden on the morning flood under the Chief Assessor's anchor in a ring. \"Low water at the bar at ten past seven, my lord. A neap, with a north wind holding the water off. Less than three feet over the bar until nearly noon. The *Gannet* drew seven. She could not have come in that morning, and the register says she did, and the copy made a week later says she didn't. Mother Agathe can call Iska a liar, and Mr Faraday blind, and Brother Hale careless. But she can't call the sea anything. The sea was there.\"\n\nThe hall was quiet. Somewhere at the back a gull was walking on the roof.\n\nPell turned to the side of the hall, where Edric Vane stood between two watchmen. He looked very old. He had been up all night in a room in the Tidegate with a clerk of the justiciar's, and his gold spectacles were folded in his breast pocket and he did not take them out.\n\n\"Chief Assessor,\" Pell said. \"Was the *Gannet* in Quell on the fifteenth of Rimefall?\"\n\n\"No, my lord. She was on the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Then why does your stamp say that she was?\"\n\nVane turned his head toward Agathe Sorrel, and everyone in the hall turned with him.\n\n\"Because Mother Agathe told me to put it there,\" he said. \"On the second of Thawmonth, my lord, in my office, with the door shut. As she told me to put the others.\"\n\nAgathe did not move. The rings on her hands lay still in her lap. When Pell asked her whether she had anything more to say, she looked at him for a long moment, and then past him, at the tall windows over the harbour, where the light lay on the water.\n\n\"Only what I said,\" she said. \"I keep a ledger. So does the sea.\"\n\nOn the way out, in the crush at the doors, Tamsin heard a watchman tell another that Captain Mell had been seen at dawn on the north road with two horses and a heavy saddlebag, and that the justiciar had sent riders after him. She found she did not much care whether they caught him.\n\nCorin came out of the Tidegate at four o'clock, when the justiciar's order reached it. Tamsin was waiting on the quay outside with Wren, and he came out blinking in the sunlight, thinner, with three days' beard and his split knuckles healed to pink, and he stood on the top step with his weight on his good leg and looked at her.\n\n\"Did you feed the child?\" he said.\n\n\"She fed me, mostly.\"\n\n\"I did,\" said Wren.\n\nThey walked back along the harbour to the Quarter, the three of them, slowly, because of his leg. The wind had dropped and the tide was making, the last big flood of the springs coming in over the mud in long gold sheets, filling the channels one by one. On the far side of the water, on the spur of rock at the harbour mouth, Tidewatch stood black and roofless against the sky. There were men on it already with ladders and a block and tackle. The Margravine had sent word: there would be a new light by the next dark of the moon.\n\n\"Who'll keep it?\" said Corin. \"There's nobody who knows the tables.\"\n\nTamsin thought of the brown calf book on the table in the Tide Court, and the forty years of others that had burned, and the old man writing by candlelight with the great lens turning over his head.\n\n\"Someone will learn,\" she said.\n\nThey came to Fishbone Walk, and the *Patience*, and there was smoke from the stovepipe, because Iska Sorrel was sitting on the cabin roof with her Guild black hitched up to her knees, fishing, badly, off the side. She looked up as they came along the Walk.\n\n\"I had nowhere else to go,\" she said. \"Is that all right?\"\n\nCorin looked at her, and at Tamsin, and at Wren, and at the boot that was still sitting on the cabin roof beside Iska, the good boot, the one that had come up on Wren's line a week ago.\n\n\"There's fish in the box,\" he said.","wordCount":1359,"createdAt":1794287774579},{"id":"pLVHpufZOxIGo6rx6KPS6","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied with tarred twine. \"She went down at the dark of last moon. A month gone. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the lowest springs. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she had expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest. And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":965,"createdAt":1794287191727},{"id":"q4QOzNJ1dhMyNlP5j8yIU","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a pistol shot, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was quite calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska didn't catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.\n\n# Breme Flats\n\n[\n  Place: #Cray's End\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Corin Dask\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Kittiwake bell:@Corin Dask\n]\n\n\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast across the flats in a long thin silver sheet the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1848,"createdAt":1794283995890},{"id":"qHWq8LSywaZSfuvUgGNgc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied around with tarred twine. \"She went down at the dark of last moon. A month gone. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the lowest springs. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest.\" He looked at her. \"And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Exchequer\n  Characters: @@Tamsin Orrey @@Edric Vane\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^Tamsin's seal:@Tamsin Orrey\n  Facts: ?The register shows the Gannet inbound after she sank:@Tamsin Orrey\n]\n\nThe Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with the Chief Assessor's mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the last voyage. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor's mark, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.\n","wordCount":2054,"createdAt":1794283904411},{"id":"rLsYoZZrEdYK_9q_z-A2V","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt was the morning of the fourth. She had slept three hours, and the Ledger Hall had expected her at eight; she had decided on the walk down to the Quarter not to go, and had not let herself think about what that would cost. It had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She stopped there too, with the lantern held close. The *Constance* had been on the mud that morning, her name freshly gilded. One letter, and a coat of paint. She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet, the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1118,"createdAt":1794294033734},{"id":"rtnya1CPaJtXzUAQcMGox","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hluy4tJm6KouKz-wCFM5S","text":"The sky had cleared by midnight. Tamsin had not seen stars over Quell since the autumn, and there were so many of them, hard and white and close, that she kept stopping on the sea stair to look up until Corin took her arm and made her keep climbing.\n\nTidewatch stood on the last spur of rock at the mouth of the inner harbour, where the estuary narrowed before it opened out toward the Teeth. It was older than the town. Nobody knew who had built it. It was a round tower of grey stone, a hundred and forty steps from the water to the lamp-room, and the sea stair wound up the outside of it, open to the wind, with an iron rail on the outer side that had rusted through in places so that you had to remember where not to lean. Corin remembered. He went first, slowly, his bad leg dragging a little on every third step, and called back the gaps to her.\n\nThe door at the top was open. Inside there was a round room full of the smell of lamp oil and old paper, and a great brass lens behind a screen, turning, throwing its light out through the windows in long slow sweeps over the water. Every few seconds the room went bright and then dim, bright and then dim. Under the lens, at a table covered in books, sat an old man in a brown robe writing by the light of a candle.\n\n\"Shut the door,\" he said, without looking up. \"Then come and sit. I'll be a moment. The tide does not wait while I make conversation.\"\n\nThey shut the door and sat on a bench by the wall. Brother Hale wrote for some minutes more, consulting a brass instrument at his elbow that looked something like a clock and something like a compass, and then he blotted the page and closed the book and turned round.\n\nHe was older than Tamsin had imagined, very thin, with a long bony face and white hair cut short as a soldier's. His hands were knotted with age and stained black at the fingertips with ink. But his eyes were clear and grey and they went straight to Corin, and then to her, and stayed on her.\n\n\"You are the girl from the Exchequer,\" he said.\n\nTamsin stared at him. \"How do you—\"\n\n\"The child told me a friend. Corin Dask has only two friends who are not lightermen, and one of them is a tide-priest, and the other one works in a building with a roof.\" He held out his hand. \"Ruth wrote to me. I've been waiting for it. Give it here.\"\n\nShe gave him the letter. He held it to the candle and looked at the seal for a long time without breaking it, and his face, which had been sharp and amused, went slack and old.\n\n\"That's her wheel,\" he said. \"That's her own seal. I gave her that ring.\" Then he broke the wax with his thumbnail and unfolded the sheets and began to read.\n\nNobody spoke. The great lens turned and the light swept round, bright and dim, bright and dim. Out on the water the tide was making; Tamsin could hear it, a low steady hush against the foot of the tower that was growing louder all the time. Brother Hale read the first sheet and the second, and then he went back and read the first again, and then he read the third and fourth slowly, with his finger under the lines.\n\nWhen he had finished he put the sheets down on the table and smoothed them flat.\n\n\"Do you want to know what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"It's not a kind thing to know.\"\n\n\"I've already been warned off it by the Chief Assessor and by Mother Agathe Sorrel,\" Tamsin said. \"In the same afternoon. I think I'd rather know.\"\n\nHe looked at her for a long moment. Then he nodded, and picked up the first sheet, and read it out to them in a dry level voice, as though it were a tide table.\n\nRuth Bessaly had sailed for the Salt Guild for twenty-two years. For the last five of them, she wrote, the Guild had been selling ships to the sea. That was what they called it among themselves. A Guild ship would be entered at the Exchequer as carrying salt, outbound, duty paid. Then it would be entered again as lost — lost at the Teeth, lost in a gale off the north coast, lost with all hands — and the Guild would claim the Crown's wreck-rebate on the duty and on the cargo both. A lost ship's cargo was insured by the Crown. That was the old law, from the days when the Margravine wanted salt to keep coming whatever the cost. The rebate on a forty-ton cargo was more than the cargo was worth.\n\nBut the ships did not sink. They were never even loaded. The salt went north on other hulls, Guild hulls under false names, and was sold untaxed in the northern markets. The ships that were entered as lost were taken out to the Flats and broken up, or renamed and sent to work somewhere they were not known. Five years. Bessaly had counted eleven ships.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*?\" said Corin.\n\nBrother Hale turned to the third sheet. \"She was to be the twelfth. They told Ruth in the autumn. She was to take the *Gannet* out to the Flats on the second of Rimefall and leave her there, and the *Gannet* would be entered as lost on the Teeth a fortnight later. Ruth refused.\" He read: \"*I told the Mother I would not do it, and she said she understood, and she was very gentle with me, and I am more frightened now than I have ever been at sea. I think they will sell me to the sea with my ship. If you get this, Brother, it will be because they have.*\"\n\nThe light swept round. Tamsin found that her hands were shaking and she held them together in her lap.\n\n\"There's more,\" Hale said. \"There's a reason she wrote to me and not to the Margravine's men. She says this.\" He turned to the last sheet and read: \"*They will enter the Gannet as having come in, after. They always do it so, to make the books balance — an inbound entry to show the cargo was never landed, so the rebate is clean. When they do, look at your tables. They are lazy and they do not look at the tide.*\"\n\nHe got up, quite quickly for an old man, and went to the shelves on the far wall, where the tide books stood in rows, forty years of them, bound in brown calf with the years stamped in gold on the spines. He took one down and opened it on the table and ran his finger down a column.\n\n\"What date is the inbound entry?\"\n\n\"The twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" Tamsin said. \"In the morning. It says on the morning flood.\"\n\nHe found the line. He looked at it, and then he turned the book round so that she could see it, and put his finger on the figure.\n\n\"Low water at the bar on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall,\" he said, \"was at ten past seven in the morning. A neap tide, and a north wind holding the water off. There was less than three feet over the bar from five o'clock until nearly noon. A sixty-ton salt barge draws seven.\" He closed the book. \"Nothing came into Quell on the morning of the twenty-fifth. Nothing could have. The *Gannet* would have been sitting on the bar with her keel in the sand, in full view of this window. I'd have seen her. I'd have written her down.\"\n\nCorin let out his breath. \"So the register's a lie.\"\n\n\"The register's a lie that can be proved,\" said Brother Hale, \"which is a different and much more dangerous thing.\" He looked at Tamsin. \"You understand that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Edric Vane stamped that entry?\"\n\n\"He says he did. He says he remembers it. He said Ruth was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"There was no fog on the twenty-fifth.\" Hale almost smiled. \"It's in the book. Clear and cold and a north wind. Edric never did look at the weather.\" He folded the letter up again, carefully, along its old creases. \"I'll keep this. It's addressed to me, and this tower has a door that locks, which is more than can be said for a lighter. You will not tell anyone you've been here. Either of you. Go down the sea stair and not the town stair. If anyone asks you where you've been tonight, you were on the *Patience* and you were drunk.\"\n\n\"And then what?\" said Tamsin. \"We know. You can prove it. And then what?\"\n\nBrother Hale looked at the letter in his hands.\n\n\"And then I'll think,\" he said. \"I'm an old man, child, and it's past midnight. Let me think.\"\n\nThey went down the sea stair with the wind behind them. Halfway down, Tamsin looked back, and saw the light still turning in the lamp-room, sweeping its long slow arm over the water, and below it, on the town side of the tower, at the foot of the town stair, a man standing very still in the shadow of the wall, with a lantern that was shuttered almost closed.\n\nShe said nothing to Corin. She was not sure, afterwards, that she had seen it at all.","wordCount":1608,"createdAt":1794286698437},{"id":"sr5F6WuXk3unBQDvdnDQn","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the start of Rimefall, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, in ballast, no duty, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the fifteenth of Rimefall, three weeks ago, sitting a little close to the line above it, as if it had been written small to fit. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the fifteenth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1062,"createdAt":1794287203852},{"id":"tSPfByIqHCkTcfMP3qWx-","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, very precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book — that it was gone. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had told him about the tide books. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1024,"createdAt":1794286710468},{"id":"uMq1PB1fiM5M5g1iY9PS3","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew.* She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning. The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in her palm and closed her fingers over it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1059,"createdAt":1794287208436},{"id":"uP7jQhM8qhrE1qbhsyKVN","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a pistol shot, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after one in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—up the town stair and then round,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"Mell's man saw them go up together, the girl and the cripple. They were up there an hour. Then they came down the other side.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was quite calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska didn't catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.\n","wordCount":780,"createdAt":1794283995763},{"id":"uYkpIKNxighuyG8VuhreJ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"mv0ZVBPQnJZX6dOA7roml","text":"Iska Sorrel had learned to listen on the stairs when she was six years old, and she had never yet been caught.\n\nIt was a matter of knowing the house. Sorrel House was three hundred years old and every board in it had its own voice, and Iska knew them all: the fourth stair that cracked like a whip, the landing board that sighed, the place outside her mother's door where if you stood on the left you were silent and if you stood on the right you might as well ring a bell. She knew that the door of the Mother's room never quite closed, because the frame had dropped in the Flood year and nobody had ever had it planed. And she knew that if you sat on the seventh stair from the top with your back against the wall, in the dark, you could hear everything said in that room as if you were sitting in it.\n\nShe was sitting there now, at a little after two in the morning, with her nightgown tucked under her feet for warmth, because Edric Vane had come to the side door twenty minutes ago and been let in by her mother herself.\n\nThat had never happened before. Her mother did not open doors.\n\n\"—followed the mudlark up there this afternoon, and sat under the town stair to see who came,\" Vane was saying. He sounded out of breath, and annoyed about it. \"They came at midnight, the girl and the cripple, round by the sea stair. They were up there an hour. Then they went down the same way.\"\n\n\"Did they bring anything away?\"\n\n\"He couldn't see. It was dark.\"\n\n\"It's always dark, Edric. That is why we pay Mell's men, so that they can see in it.\" Her mother's voice was calm. It was the voice she used for bad news about cargo prices. \"And the old man has the letter now. Bessaly's letter.\"\n\n\"We don't know there is a letter.\"\n\n\"There is always a letter. There was a letter with Penhallow, five years ago, and you didn't believe me then either. I had the boy fish it out of the dock with a boathook in the end.\" A pause. The click of rings. \"Hale will go to the Margravine's justiciar. Not tomorrow. He'll wait, he's a cautious old creature. He'll want to be sure. He'll go through his books. A week, perhaps.\"\n\n\"Agathe, if Hale has his tables—\"\n\n\"Then the tower must have a bad night.\" Very calmly. \"Old towers do. Lamp oil, and old paper, and an old man who sleeps in his chair. Mell will see to it. Not yet. When the girl has gone back to her desk and stopped looking over her shoulder.\"\n\nOn the seventh stair Iska put her hand over her own mouth and held it there.\n\n\"And the girl?\" said Vane.\n\n\"The girl's yours. She's your clerk. Frighten her. Take her seal, if you have to. Give her a reason to be quiet that she can live with.\" The rings again. \"And Edric — the second book. I want it out of this house. I don't want it here if the justiciar comes knocking with a warrant. Take it to the Exchequer and put it in the flood vault, where nobody can get at it without your key and the spring tide.\"\n\n\"Tonight?\"\n\n\"Next spring tide. Four days. Until then it stays in my box, and the key stays round my neck, where it's been for twenty years.\"\n\nVane said something Iska did not catch. Her mother laughed, a dry small sound like a dead leaf.\n\n\"I'm sixty-eight years old, Edric. I sleep very soundly. Go home.\"\n\nIska went up the stairs before the door opened, on the left side, in the dark, and got into bed with her heart going like a hammer and lay looking at the ceiling. She heard Vane go down, and the side door, and her mother's slow steps along the landing, and the door that never quite closed.\n\nShe knew the box. She had known it all her life: a small iron strongbox under her mother's bed, with brass corners and a lock shaped like a scallop shell. She knew the key, too, on its chain, warm from her mother's skin. When she was little she had been allowed to play with it while her mother told her stories, and she remembered the weight of it in her palm, and the way the wards were cut in the shape of waves.\n\nShe waited until the bells of the Saltmarket had struck three. Then she got up and went along the landing, on the left side, and pushed the door that never quite closed, and went in.","wordCount":791,"createdAt":1794287231080},{"id":"ubdSl0voJXo-J0JHSBkfD","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hgppZV17-OgnRLsnrPzBR","text":"The spring tide came at dawn on the seventh of Thawmonth, and Tamsin Orrey went down to meet it with a dead man's key round her neck and her own seal back on its cord beside it.\n\nShe had not slept. She had spent the day after the fire in her rooms with the shutters closed, while the town talked about the old priest who had knocked over his lamp, and the watch put up a notice on the Saltmarket Cross offering a reward for information about the lighterman Dask's accomplices in the matter of the *Gannet*, and Wren went in and out with bread and news. Nobody came for her. That frightened her more than if they had. It meant Vane was content to wait. It meant he thought she had nothing.\n\nThe flood vault was older than the Exchequer. The building had been put up on top of it two hundred years ago, but the vault itself had been cut into the rock of the hill by whoever had built Tidewatch, in the same stone. It was where the Crown's copies were kept. Every register entry made in Quell was copied twice, once into the green book on the lectern and once onto loose sheets, copied in the hall by old Faraday, that went down into the vault at every spring tide, sealed, to be sent to the Margravine's treasury at Hesk at the year's end. The vault could be opened only at the spring tides, when the sea came up the old culvert under the hill and lifted the great iron counterweight that held the door. Two keys. One for the Exchequer and one for the tower. So that no one man could.\n\nHobb was in his box when she came in by the side door at half past five. He looked at her, and at the seal on its cord, two hundred and six, and she saw him remember that she had been suspended, and then she saw him decide that it was half past five in the morning and that he had not been told.\n\n\"Early, miss.\"\n\n\"The count,\" she said. \"Spring tide.\"\n\n\"So it is.\" He went back to his newspaper.\n\nThe stair down to the vault was behind a door at the back of the archive room, and the door was not locked, because there was no need. At the bottom of the stair there was a short passage cut in the rock, wet and cold and smelling of the sea, and at the end of the passage there was the vault door: a slab of black iron eight feet high, with two keyholes in it side by side, and above it, on a great chain running up into the dark, the counterweight.\n\nShe could hear the water. It was below her somewhere, in the rock, booming and sucking in the culvert. As she stood there it grew louder. Then the chain above the door gave a groan and a shudder, and the counterweight began, very slowly, to rise.\n\nShe put Hale's key into the right-hand keyhole. It turned as if it had been oiled yesterday.\n\nThe left-hand one was Vane's. She had known that, and she had come anyway, because she had thought — she had not let herself think it clearly, but she had thought it — that Vane would come. That he had to bring the second book down and put it in the vault on this tide, as Agathe had told him to, and that he did not yet know the book was gone. That Mell had gone up to the tower for Hale's key and come down without it, and that Vane would know by now that whoever had it would be here at the spring tide. That he would come down the stair with his key, and find her.\n\nShe heard his step on the stair behind her.\n\n\"Miss Orrey,\" said Edric Vane.\n\nHe was in his greatcoat with a lantern, and he was alone, and he had a bundle under his arm wrapped in oilskin, the same size and shape as the book Iska had brought to the chapel. Tamsin looked at it, and could not help herself; she laughed.\n\n\"What?\" He sounded, for the first time since she had known him, entirely at a loss. \"What is it?\"\n\n\"It's psalms,\" she said. \"Sir. Unwrap it. It's a book of psalms.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bundle. He did not unwrap it. She watched him understand, the way she had watched him understand everything, for three years, through the glass door, and never once seen it cost him anything; and she watched him look at the key in the right-hand lock, the wave-shaped bow, and understand that too.\n\n\"Hale,\" he said. \"Mell swore it wasn't on him.\"\n\n\"Hale's dead. You know he is. You sent Mell.\"\n\n\"I didn't send anybody.\" His voice cracked on it. \"I didn't — that was her. I told her not to. I told her the tower was too much.\" He put the lantern down on the floor. His hands were shaking. \"You don't understand. Twenty years, Tamsin. She had me twenty years ago. A gambling debt and a letter I shouldn't have written. Twenty years, and everything I've stamped since, she's had a hand on it.\"\n\n\"Open the door, sir.\"\n\n\"Why should I?\"\n\n\"Because the Crown's copies are in there. The fortnight with the fifteenth in it went down at the springs on the twenty-second, sealed. If the *Gannet* isn't on that sheet, then she was written into the green book afterwards and dated back, and it's your hand and your anchor.\" She took a breath. \"That's you finished, sir, whatever I do. I can't help that. But you're the only one who can say who told you to write it. Open the door, and say it to the justiciar, and it isn't only you.\"\n\nHe stood looking at her for a long time. Behind the door the sea boomed in the rock, and the counterweight hung at the top of its chain.\n\nThen he took his key out of his waistcoat pocket and put it in the left-hand lock and turned it.\n\nThe door swung inward on a breath of cold salt air. Inside was a small round chamber lined with iron shelves, and on the shelves, in sealed bundles with their dates written on the outside in red ink, the Crown's copies. Hundreds of them. She found the quarter she wanted in a minute and broke the seal with her thumbnail, the way she had seen Brother Hale break Ruth Bessaly's wax, and leafed through the sheets by Vane's lantern.\n\n*Gannet. Breme. Master R. Bessaly. Outbound 2 Rimefall.*\n\nAnd after that, nothing. No inbound entry. Whoever had copied that quarter's sheets had copied the true register, before the lie had been written into it — or had not been told.\n\nShe held the sheet up. Vane looked at it. He sat down slowly on the wet stone step, with his back against the wall and his useless bundle of psalms on his knees.\n\n\"Faraday copied that quarter,\" he said. \"He was always slow. He was always weeks behind.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir,\" said Tamsin. \"He told me once. The only way to read a register is all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe water came up through the floor of the passage behind them, an inch, two inches, and the counterweight began to groan on its chain. She took the sheets, and took his arm, and got him up the stair before the door swung shut.","wordCount":1265,"createdAt":1794287269002},{"id":"vEkRlkdiro3xgWiFbpmYU","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet* was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew. She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the fifteenth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her mast there myself on the tenth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning. The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in her palm and closed her fingers over it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1060,"createdAt":1794294030569},{"id":"vjnzBh9vZG5neOguiqCR1","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"SiE3-6R3cGh7qe6M9RAV_","text":"\"She said not to go on my own,\" Tamsin said. \"I'm not on my own.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\" Corin leaned on the pole and the lighter slid out from under the shadow of the causeway into the open water of the estuary. \"You'd make a good Guild mother.\"\n\n\"Take that back.\"\n\n\"I'll take it back if we get home.\"\n\nIt had rained since dawn, a heavy grey rain that flattened the water and blotted out the far shore, and they were both soaked before they had passed the last of the Quarter's chimneys. Tamsin sat in the bow with her shawl over her head and her notebook buttoned inside her coat and watched the Flats come up out of the rain ahead of them: a long low country, neither land nor sea, mile after mile of mud and marsh-grass and shining water, laid out in squares like a chessboard where the Guild had built its salt pans.\n\nThe Breme saltworks had been there for two hundred years. The pans were shallow pools walled with clay, a hundred feet on a side, and at the spring tides the Guild let the sea in through sluices and then shut it in and let the sun and wind take the water away and leave the salt behind. In summer the whole Flats glittered white. Now, in Thawmonth, in the rain, it was all grey, grey water in grey pans, and the long low sheds where the salt was stored stood up out of it like the backs of drowned animals.\n\n\"Where?\" Corin said.\n\n\"Bessaly said they break them up on the Flats. You don't break up a ship in a salt pan. You'd need a slip. Somewhere with hard ground.\"\n\n\"There's the old boatyard at Cray's End. Guild's had it shut for years.\" He pointed with his chin. \"There. Past the last sheds.\"\n\nThey tied up at the end of a rotten jetty and walked. The ground was mud with a skin of shingle over it that gave under their boots. Nobody was about. Tamsin had thought there would be saltworkers, but the pans were empty and still in the rain and the only sound was the wind and the sucking of their feet. Corin went slowly. Once his bad leg went into a soft place up to the knee and she had to haul him out by the collar, and they both stood there swearing and laughing in the rain.\n\nThe boatyard at Cray's End was a long shed with its seaward end open to a slipway of black timbers. The doors on the land side were chained, but the chain went round a post that had rotted at the base, and Corin lifted the whole post out of the ground and they went in.\n\nIt was dark inside, and smelled of tar and wet sawdust and something else, an old cold smell like a church. As her eyes adjusted, Tamsin saw that the shed was full of timber, stacked to the roof along both walls: ribs and planks and knees, masts with the rigging still on them, rudders, hatch-covers, a ship's wheel leaning against a post. All of it sorted. All of it neatly stacked and ready to be used again.\n\nAnd along the back wall, propped one beside the other like gravestones, the nameboards.\n\nShe walked down the line of them with her lantern. They were long curved boards from the sterns of ships, carved and painted, most of them faded and some of them freshly gilded. *Kittiwake. Merrow. Sweet Annie. Dorcas Bell. The Honest Woman.* She stopped at that one for a while. *Seaholly. Plover. Constant.* She took out her notebook with wet fingers and wrote them down, one by one, while Corin held the lantern.\n\nEleven. Bessaly had counted eleven.\n\nThere was a twelfth board at the end of the line, newer than the rest, and blank. Someone had planed the old name off it and not yet painted a new one. In the raw wood, if she held the lantern at a slant, she could just see the ghost of the letters where the paint had soaked in. G and a run of letters and a T.\n\n\"They were going to bring her here,\" she said. \"They were ready for her.\"\n\n\"And she wouldn't come.\" Corin's voice was rough. \"So they put her on the Teeth instead.\"\n\nHe had picked up something from the floor by the wheel: a small brass bell, green with salt, with a name stamped round the lip. He turned it so she could read it. *Kittiwake*.\n\n\"Put it down.\"\n\n\"It's proof.\"\n\n\"It's a bell. Put it down, Corin, we've got the names, that's enough—\"\n\nSomebody shouted outside.\n\nThey both froze. The shout came again, closer, from the land side, and a dog began to bark. Through a gap in the planking Tamsin saw lanterns coming along the dyke from the sheds, three of them, four, and men behind them, big men in the oilskins and clogs the saltworkers wore.\n\n\"The slip,\" said Corin. \"Go.\"\n\nThey went down the slipway at a run into the rain. The tide was coming in. It had been out when they arrived, the mud bare and shining to the edge of the channel, and now there was water over the mud, an inch, two inches, moving in fast in a long thin silver sheet the way the tide moved on the Flats, faster than a man could walk. They ran through it. Behind them the dog was going mad on the slipway and somebody was shouting *stop, you, stop there*, and a lantern swung out over the water.\n\nThey reached the lighter with the water up to their shins. Corin threw her in and fell in after her, and pushed off with the pole, and the channel took them, and the rain closed behind them like a door.\n\nIt was not until they were halfway back across the estuary, shaking and soaked and with the Quarter's chimneys coming up out of the rain ahead of them, that she saw he still had the bell. He had it inside his coat. He looked at her, and his chin came up, and he did not say anything, and she did not either.","wordCount":1044,"createdAt":1794286704135},{"id":"w9Hh9qV7Pik3adcB87Whv","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"a2o-bgDW5fV3EL8dB2zkm","text":"The *Patience* had been Corin's father's barge, and his grandfather's before that, and it had been moored at the end of Fishbone Walk in the Drowned Quarter for as long as Tamsin could remember.\n\nThe Quarter was what had been left of the lower town after the Great Flood eighty years ago. The sea had come up and not gone back. Half the streets were canals now, and the other half were canals at high water, and the people who lived there lived upstairs and kept boats tied to their window-sills. Tamsin had grown up three houses along from the Dasks. She had learned to swim before she learned to read. She still could not walk down Fishbone Walk at night without smelling her mother's kitchen.\n\nThe rain had eased by the time she got there, to a fine drifting mist that hung in the lamplight over the water. There was a light in the *Patience*'s cabin, and smoke from the stovepipe, and a small figure sitting on the cabin roof with its knees drawn up and a fishing line over the side.\n\n\"He's inside,\" said the figure. \"You're late. He said you'd be late.\"\n\n\"Hello, Wren.\"\n\nWren was ten, or eleven, or possibly nine; nobody knew, least of all Wren. She had turned up in the Quarter two winters ago, sleeping under the Fishbone bridge, and Corin had started leaving food for her, and now she slept on the *Patience* most nights and ran messages for anyone who paid her and fished off the roof when no one did. She had a pointed face and short black hair she cut herself with a knife.\n\n\"Caught anything?\"\n\n\"An eel. Then it got away. Then a boot.\" Wren pointed at a boot on the roof beside her. \"It's a good boot. It's just the one.\"\n\nTamsin went down the ladder into the cabin. It was low and warm and smelled of tar and wet wool and fried onions. Corin was at the stove with a pan, and he looked round as she came in, and for a moment his face did something she did not have a word for. Then he grinned.\n\n\"You came.\"\n\n\"I said I would.\"\n\n\"You say a lot of things.\" He pointed the spatula at the bench. \"Sit. Eat. Then show me.\"\n\nShe ate. Onions and bread and a piece of smoked fish, and a mug of something hot that had a good deal of rum in it. Wren came down halfway through and ate the rest of the fish out of the pan with her fingers. Then Tamsin took the oilskin packet out of her waistcoat and put it on the table, and the three of them looked at it.\n\n\"I didn't show anyone,\" she said. \"But the only person I asked about the *Gannet was Vane, and three hours later Mother Agathe knew.* She asked me about it straight out.\"\n\nCorin was quiet. Then he said, \"Vane told her.\"\n\n\"I think so.\"\n\n\"So they talk.\"\n\n\"The Exchequer and the Guild talk all the time, Corin. That's the whole—\" She stopped. \"No. You're right. It was too fast. He'd have had to send to her the minute I left his office.\"\n\nShe told him about the register then, the *Gannet* coming in on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall with Bessaly at the helm, stamped by Vane's own hand, and Corin's face went still.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said. \"She was on the Teeth by then. I saw her there myself before the twentieth. I saw her mast.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" She untied the twine. Her fingers were stiff and it took a long time. Inside the oilskin there was a second layer, waxed cloth, and inside that a letter, three or four sheets folded together into a thick packet and sealed with a great blob of green wax. The seal had been pressed with something that left the shape of a ship's wheel. On the outside, in brown ink that had run a little at the edges where the water had got in after all, it said again: *To the Lamp. By hand.*\n\n\"It's wet,\" Wren said.\n\n\"It's damp.\" Tamsin turned it toward the stove. \"If we dry it slow it'll be all right.\"\n\n\"Are we going to open it?\" said Wren.\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin and Corin together.\n\nCorin rubbed his face. \"It's not ours. It's for the Lamp. That's what I wanted to tell you this morning.\" He looked at her. \"The old sailors on the Stair, when they talk about the Lamp, they mean the tower. Tidewatch. They mean the old man who keeps the light. Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"The tide-priest?\"\n\n\"He's kept the tables for forty years. Every tide, high and low, every day, written down. Ruth Bessaly knew him. Everyone who ever crossed the bar knew him.\" He touched the edge of the letter, very lightly, with one split knuckle. \"She knew she was going to drown, Tam. She nailed this under her chart table because she knew. And she wrote it to him.\"\n\nThey dried the letter by the stove, turning it every few minutes like a piece of toast, until the paper was stiff and crackled and the brown ink had stopped spreading. Then Tamsin wrapped it up again in the waxed cloth and put it back inside her waistcoat.\n\n\"Wren,\" Corin said. \"Do you know the way to Tidewatch?\"\n\n\"Course I know the way to Tidewatch.\"\n\n\"Go up there now and find the brother, and tell him that Corin Dask from the *Patience* and a friend want to see him tonight about Ruth Bessaly. Just that. Don't say anything else, not to him and not to anyone you meet on the way. Can you do that?\"\n\nWren held out her hand. Tamsin found a copper in her pocket and put it in it, and Wren looked at the copper and then at her and then put it away somewhere in her clothes and was gone up the ladder without another word.\n\nShe came back two hours later, soaked to the skin, with her hair standing up in wet spikes. The brother would see them. He said to come at the night tide, when the light was lit, and to come up the sea stair and not the town stair, and to bring nobody else.","wordCount":1057,"createdAt":1794286695903},{"id":"wa4bO42wIXTrykLmKntz7","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"h5QNc3cCqfoMQ3uBbVVxu","text":"Iska Sorrel found her at the chapel of St Brannoc that evening, which Tamsin would not have thought was possible, since nobody but the Quarter's own people even knew the chapel was there.\n\nIt stood at the far end of Fishbone Walk where the Walk ran out into open water. The Flood had taken its lower half eighty years ago and never given it back, so that now you came to it by boat and climbed in through what had been the clerestory windows, and stood on a gallery above a nave full of black water, with the tops of the pews just showing and the painted saints on the walls up to their chests in the sea, and in the middle of the nave a stone St Brannoc standing in the water to his chest, with one hand lifted as if to keep something dry. The Quarter's children came here to dare each other to swim the nave. The Quarter's old women came here to pray.\n\nTamsin came here when she wanted to be somewhere nobody from the Exchequer would ever find her. She had been sitting on the gallery for an hour with her wet notebook drying on her knee when she heard someone climbing in through the window behind her.\n\n\"Your messenger,\" Iska said, \"is very expensive.\"\n\n\"Wren?\"\n\n\"She wanted a silver crown to tell me where you were. I gave her two, to make sure she'd forget me after.\" Iska sat down on the gallery beside her. She was wearing a plain brown cloak over her Guild black, and her boots were wet to the ankle, and she was carrying a bundle wrapped in sacking under her arm. \"I said don't go to the Flats.\"\n\n\"I didn't go alone.\"\n\n\"That's a lawyer's answer.\"\n\nTamsin laughed — she couldn't help it — and Iska stared at her as if she had lost her mind.\n\n\"Sorry. It's — somebody said that to me this morning.\" She sobered. \"How do you know I went?\"\n\n\"Because the foreman at Cray's End sent a boy to my mother at noon to say that two people had been in the boatyard, a woman and a man with a lame leg. My mother was very calm about it. My mother is always very calm.\" Iska looked down at the black water under the gallery. \"She calls it selling ships to the sea. Did you know that? She's done it for five years. She used to say it at dinner, when I was younger, as a joke. *We've sold another one to the sea, Iska.* I thought she meant they'd sunk.\"\n\n\"Some of them did,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"Yes.\" Iska did not look up. \"I know that now.\"\n\nShe put the bundle on Tamsin's knees. It was heavy and square. Tamsin unwrapped the sacking and found a ledger, an ordinary account book bound in plain brown board, with nothing written on the cover.\n\n\"It's the second book,\" said Iska. \"The true one. Every ship they've sold, every rebate, who was paid and how much. My mother has kept it since the start. She keeps everything. She thinks if she writes a thing down it belongs to her.\" She drew a breath. \"Edric Vane's name is in it on every page. So is Mell's. Captain Mell, of the watch.\"\n\nTamsin opened it. The pages were close-written in a small, upright, old-fashioned hand, with sums in the margins. She turned to the back and found the last entry: *Gannet. To the Teeth. 2 Rimefall. R.B. not to be paid*.\n\nShe closed the book. Her hands were not quite steady.\n\n\"How did you get it?\"\n\n\"I took it out of the box under her bed while she was asleep. Last night. Three in the morning.\" Iska's mouth twisted. \"She sleeps very soundly. She told him so.\"\n\n\"Told who?\"\n\nSo Iska told her. About Edric Vane at the side door, and the seventh stair, and the tower that must have a bad night. About Mell's man at the foot of the town stair. About Vane's orders: frighten the girl, take her seal. About the flood vault and the spring tide in four days, three now.\n\n\"She said not yet,\" Iska said. \"She said she'd wait until you'd stopped looking over your shoulder. But that was before I took the book. When she finds it's gone, she won't wait.\"\n\n\"Has she found it?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I put a book of psalms in the box in its place. The same size.\" Iska almost smiled. \"She doesn't read psalms.\"\n\nTamsin sat with the ledger on her knees, and the black water of the drowned nave below her, and the painted saints looking up at her out of the sea.\n\n\"I have to warn him,\" she said. \"Brother Hale.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Tonight. I have to go up there tonight.\"\n\n\"They'll be watching the tower,\" Iska said. \"Mell's men. Both stairs. If you go up there tonight they'll know you've been warned, and then they'll know someone warned you, and there are only so many people who could have.\" She looked at Tamsin, finally. \"Send the child in the morning. Nobody watches a mudlark. And Tamsin — keep that book somewhere they won't look. Not the Exchequer. Not your rooms.\"\n\n\"Where, then?\"\n\nIska stood up. She pulled her brown cloak round her, over the Guild black.\n\n\"Somewhere you'd never go,\" she said. \"I'd put it in a church.\"","wordCount":904,"createdAt":1794287243251},{"id":"wcIbu1hxpEEd7Cyt4xQOX","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"Hv8l79Q3Vq20p2tYxVeeE","text":"The fog came off the estuary in the hour before dawn and lay on the Long Quay like wet wool, and Tamsin Orrey counted hulls by the sound of them.\n\nYou could do it, if you had done it long enough. A salt-barge on the mud at low water made a different noise from a coaster: a deep, patient creak, timbers settling against their own weight, where a coaster ticked and dripped like a clock running down. Three years of low-tide counts had taught her the difference. Old Faraday, who had trained her, claimed he could tell a ship's home port by the way its rigging sang. Tamsin did not believe him, but she had never caught him out either.\n\nShe walked the edge of the quay with her slate under her arm and her lantern held low, so the light fell on the mud and not back into her eyes. Every few yards she stopped, found a hull with the lantern, found the painted name on its bow, and chalked it. Eleven so far. The *Marigold*, the *Constance*, the *Third Daughter*, two Guild barges with their names freshly gilded and their decks heaped with tarpaulins. The cold got into her left hand, her chalk hand, and she flexed it inside the glove and kept going.\n\n\"You'll fall in one day,\" said a voice below her, \"and I'll be the one who has to fish you out.\"\n\nShe lowered the lantern. A flat-bottomed lighter had come up the channel without her hearing it, which meant whoever was poling it knew the mud better than she knew the hulls. The man at the pole was tall and bareheaded, his hair plastered to his skull with fog, and he stood with his weight on his good leg the way he had since he was fifteen.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" she said. \"You're on the wrong side of the quay. The lighter berths are at the Stair.\"\n\n\"I know where the lighter berths are.\" He shipped the pole and the boat drifted the last yard and bumped against the stones. \"I came to find you. They said at the Exchequer you'd be on the count.\"\n\n\"Who said?\"\n\n\"The porter. Fat man. Smells of cloves.\"\n\n\"Hobb.\" She crouched at the edge so they were nearer of a height. Close to, he looked worse than the fog accounted for. There were dark half-moons under his eyes, and the knuckles of his right hand were split. \"What happened to you?\"\n\n\"I've been out past the Teeth.\"\n\nTamsin said nothing for a moment. The Teeth were the line of rocks across the mouth of the estuary, three miles out, where the bar shoaled and the current turned and ships that misjudged the tide went down without anyone on shore hearing a thing. Nobody went out past the Teeth in a lighter. Lighters were for the harbour. They were barely for the harbour.\n\n\"Why,\" she said.\n\n\"There's a wreck there. You'll know it. The *Gannet*, out of Breme.\" He reached into the front of his coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilskin, the oilskin black and slick and tied with tarred twine. \"She went down at the dark of last moon. A month gone. Ruth Bessaly was her master. You remember Ruth? She used to give us oranges, on the Stair, when her ship came in.\"\n\n\"I remember.\" She had been nine. Bessaly had been a broad red woman with a voice like a foghorn and a gold tooth she let children touch for luck. \"She drowned?\"\n\n\"All hands, they say. Nobody's found anyone.\" He held out the packet. \"The stern's out of the water at the lowest springs. I went out at slack last night and got into her cabin. This was nailed under the chart table. Nailed, Tam. Somebody wanted it found by somebody who knew where to look.\"\n\nShe took it. The oilskin was cold and heavier than she had expected. Through it she could feel the stiff edges of folded paper, and a round hard lump that was probably a seal.\n\n\"And you brought it to me,\" she said, \"because—\"\n\n\"Because it's addressed, and I can read that much, and I can't read the rest.\" He looked at her. \"And because you're the only person I know who works in a building with a roof.\"\n\nShe turned the packet over. Someone had written on the outside of the oilskin in tar, in big careful letters: TO THE LAMP. BY HAND.\n\n\"What lamp?\"\n\n\"I've an idea,\" Corin said. \"But I wanted you to look first. You're the clever one.\"\n\n\"I'm the one with a job to do,\" she said, but she put the packet inside her coat, against her ribs, where it lay cold and flat. \"I've nine more hulls to count and then I'm due at the Ledger Hall. Come and find me tonight.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Wherever you're tied up.\"\n\n\"The *Patience* is at the Quarter. Same as ever.\" He picked up his pole. \"Tam. Don't show it to anyone at your Exchequer.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\nBut he had already pushed off, and the fog closed over the lighter, and the last she saw of him was the pale shape of his hands on the pole, going away down the channel toward the Drowned Quarter.\n\nShe finished the count. Twenty hulls on the mud on the morning of the third of Thawmonth, and every one of them she could name. She stood for a while at the end of the quay, where the fog was beginning to thin and the first grey light showed the shape of the Exchequer roof on the hill above the town, and she thought about Ruth Bessaly's gold tooth, and the dark of last moon, and the cold weight inside her coat.\n\nThen she went up the hill to work.","wordCount":969,"createdAt":1794286687036},{"id":"xQjadvRZxrufgfQQttmjZ","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior assessors did not make entries. Junior assessors were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the start of Rimefall, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, in ballast, no duty, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the fifteenth of Rimefall, eighteen days ago, sitting a little close to the line above it, as if it had been written small to fit. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. She looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the fifteenth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the fifteenth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1059,"createdAt":1794294081794},{"id":"x_sMiUk3qft-qVzvDKZ-j","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor. She had lain awake working it out. Vane wanted her quiet, and Mell would do what Vane told him. If she could make Vane afraid enough of what she knew, he might buy her quiet with Corin.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer had entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1048,"createdAt":1794287253776},{"id":"zV7pk8CcRPkN-5V5X_jpL","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"M-MibIvLziUtQmqIEL3f7","text":"They came for Corin a little after midnight.\n\nTamsin was on the *Patience* when they did. She had not meant to be; she had meant to go home to her rooms on Lantern Stair and sleep, but she had walked back along Fishbone Walk from the chapel in the dark with nothing in her arms. The ledger was behind her, wrapped in oilskin and wedged up inside the hollow of St Brannoc's lifted hand, where only a swimmer could reach it. When she came to the barge there had been a light in the cabin and she had gone down.\n\nCorin was mending a net. Wren was asleep on the bench under a pile of coats. He did not ask where she had been. He gave her the rest of the rum and she drank it and told him about Iska and the ledger, and he listened without saying anything, and when she had finished he said, \"Good,\" and went back to his net.\n\nThe first Tamsin knew of them was the barge tilting. Somebody heavy had stepped onto the deck. Then another. Then the hatch was pulled back and a lantern was shoved down into the cabin, and behind it a face she knew: red and square, with a sandy moustache, under the tall leather cap of the Harbour Watch.\n\n\"Corin Dask,\" said Captain Oszkar Mell. \"Up you come.\"\n\nCorin put down the net. \"What for?\"\n\n\"Wrecking.\" Mell came down the ladder. He was a big man and the cabin was suddenly very small. Two more watchmen came down behind him. \"You were seen on the wreck of the *Gannet* two nights ago, taking goods out of her. The Guild's laid a complaint. The foreman at Cray's End says a lame man carried something out of his yard this morning.\" He looked round the cabin, and saw Tamsin, and his eyes rested on her a moment longer than they needed to. \"Evening, miss.\"\n\n\"He didn't take anything,\" Tamsin said. \"He went out to look, that's all, that's not a crime—\"\n\n\"Is it not.\" Mell crossed to Corin's coat on its hook and put his hand into the pocket and brought out the little green bell. *Kittiwake*. \"What's this, then?\"\n\n\"That's not from the *Gannet*,\" Tamsin said, and then stopped, because Mell was smiling, and she understood a second too late what she had just told him.\n\n\"No? Where's it from, then, miss?\"\n\nNobody said anything. On the bench Wren had woken and was lying very still under the coats with her eyes open.\n\n\"Bring him,\" said Mell.\n\nCorin went without a fight. He stood up and put his coat on and went up the ladder with a watchman in front and one behind, and on the deck he turned and looked back down at her through the hatch.\n\n\"Feed the child,\" he said. \"There's fish in the box.\"\n\nThen they took him along the Walk toward the Tidegate Gaol, with the lanterns bobbing, and Tamsin stood on the deck of the *Patience* in the dark and watched them until they went round the corner, and Wren came and stood beside her and put a cold hand in hers and did not say anything at all.","wordCount":536,"createdAt":1794287250767},{"id":"zZOFRaVny3iKZIa9h74_p","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"_VAbokfKMS84F9UgL3MNU","text":"She sent Wren up to Tidewatch at first light with a message she made her repeat three times — *the tower is to have a bad night, leave it, come down* — and then she washed her face in the canal and went up the hill to the Exchequer and asked to see the Chief Assessor.\n\nHe kept her waiting an hour. She sat on the bench outside the glass door and watched him through it, writing at his desk by the fire with his back to her, and the forty clerks in the Ledger Hall bent over their desks and did not look at her, any of them, in a way that told her they all knew she was there.\n\nWhen he called her in, he did not get up.\n\n\"Shut the door, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe shut it. It was warm in his office. She had never been in it before. There was a carpet, and a second chair by the fire, and a picture of the Margravine over the mantel looking down her long nose at the room. His desk was very tidy. There was a silver inkstand, and a blotter, and his seal on its stand, the anchor in a ring.\n\n\"Captain Mell tells me,\" Vane said, \"that a friend of yours was taken up last night for wrecking.\"\n\n\"Corin Dask didn't wreck anything, sir.\"\n\n\"He had goods from a wrecked ship in his coat. In front of a witness. In front of two witnesses, I'm told.\" He took off his spectacles and looked at her. \"Do you know what the penalty is for wrecking, Miss Orrey? In Quell, under the old law?\"\n\nShe did. Everyone in the Quarter did. They had hanged a man on the Tidegate for it when she was twelve and left him there through two tides.\n\n\"He found that bell at Cray's End,\" she said. \"In a boatyard the Guild's had shut for years. Along with the nameboards of eleven ships that the Exchequer entered as lost at sea.\" Her voice was shaking; she let it. \"I have the names. I can give you the names, sir, and you can look them up in the register, and you'll find your stamp on every one of them.\"\n\nVane did not move. He sat looking at her with his spectacles in his hand.\n\n\"And the *Gannet*, sir. You stamped her inbound on the morning of the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. On a neap tide, with a north wind. There was less than three feet of water over the bar. She draws seven. You said Ruth Bessaly was in a temper about the fog, but there wasn't any fog.\" She made herself say it. \"It's in the tide books, sir. At Tidewatch.\"\n\nFor a long moment there was no sound but the fire.\n\nThen Vane put his spectacles down on the blotter, precisely, and opened the drawer of his desk, and took out a sheet of paper already written on and signed.\n\n\"This is an order suspending you from the service of the Exchequer,\" he said, \"for conduct unbecoming. Pending inquiry. You'll leave your seal with me.\" He held out his hand.\n\nShe looked at it. Every assessor had a seal. Hers was small and brass, on a cord round her neck, with her number cut in it — two hundred and six — and nothing else; a junior's seal, good for countersigning counts and nothing more. She had been so proud of it the day they gave it to her that she had slept with it in her hand.\n\n\"Your seal, Miss Orrey.\"\n\nShe took the cord over her head and put the seal in his palm. He closed his fingers on it and put it in the drawer and shut the drawer.\n\n\"Now listen to me,\" he said. \"Because I am going to say this once, and I am saying it because I liked your Mr Faraday, and he liked you. Corin Dask is in the Tidegate. Whether he stays there until the assize, and what happens to him at the assize, is a matter on which Captain Mell is willing to be guided by this office. Do you understand me?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"The old man in the tower is very old. Old men have accidents. You will not go up there again.\" He put his spectacles back on. \"And you have nothing. You have a list of names on wet paper, and a tide book in a tower, and a lighterman in gaol for theft. You have no ledger, and no letter, and as of this morning, no seal. Go home, Miss Orrey. Stay there. In a month, perhaps, we'll talk about your coming back.\"\n\nShe went. She went out through the glass door and down the length of the Ledger Hall between the two rows of high desks, with forty clerks not looking at her, and down the stairs, and past Hobb the porter in his box, who smelled of cloves and said *Morning, miss* as if nothing had happened, and out into the street.\n\nHe had said no ledger. He had said no letter. He did not know that Iska had the book — that it was gone. And he thought Hale still had the letter, or he would not have said *old men have accidents* in that voice.\n\nShe stood in the street and the wind went through her. She had told him about the tide books. She had stood in his warm office and told him, in so many words, exactly what the old man in the tower could prove.\n\nWren came round the corner at a run.\n\n\"He won't come,\" she said, before Tamsin could speak. \"I told him. Three times, like you said. He just said thank you and gave me this and said give it you.\" She held out a book bound in brown calf, with a year stamped on the spine in gold. \"He says it's the one that matters. He says he'll keep the letter where it is. He says the tower's stood eight hundred years and it's not going to fall down for Agathe Sorrel.\"","wordCount":1023,"createdAt":1794286711737},{"id":"zgE2tgYA3YsUIhz-iNpXc","worldId":"UAX__qUt9OL_Vk9KpTsuf","eventId":"hH64PSzEtVFkFyft9kANP","text":"The Ledger Hall of the Harbour Exchequer was the largest room Tamsin had ever been inside, and she had never once been warm in it.\n\nIt ran the whole length of the building's upper floor, under a roof of black beams so high that the lamps hung from them on chains that swung in the draughts. Forty clerks sat at two rows of high desks. The fire at the far end was lit every morning at seven and was for the Chief Assessor's office, which opened off the hall through a glass door, and none of its heat had ever been known to reach the desks.\n\nShe hung her wet coat on its hook, with the oilskin packet still inside it, and then went back and took the packet out and put it in the inside pocket of her waistcoat instead, where it made an awkward square bulge she covered with her shawl. Then she sat down, copied her count from the slate into the day-book, and went to the registers.\n\nThe hull register for the current quarter stood on its own lectern by the window. It was a great thing, bound in green leather, its pages ruled in red, and every ship that crossed the bar into Quell and paid duty to the Crown was entered in it: name, master, home port, tonnage, cargo, duty paid and the date. The entries were made by the assessors in the Chief Assessor's office and stamped with his mark. Junior clerks did not make entries. Junior clerks were allowed to read them.\n\nTamsin read.\n\nShe went back four weeks first, to the dark of last moon, and found the *Gannet* where she had expected to find her. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons, entered on the second of Rimefall. Outbound for the north coast that same tide. That was the voyage she had never come back from.\n\nThen, because she was thorough, and because Faraday had told her once that the only way to read a register was all the way to the end, she kept turning.\n\nThe *Gannet* was there again on the twenty-fifth of Rimefall. Eight days ago. Gannet, Breme, master R. Bessaly, sixty tons, salt in bulk, duty paid on forty tons. Inbound. And beside it, in the margin, the round red stamp of the Chief Assessor, an anchor inside a ring.\n\nShe read it three times. She put her finger on the line, as though the ink might come away on it. Then she looked up and down the page for any sign of a correction, a crossing out, a note in the margin saying *entered in error*, and there was nothing. The entry was in the same neat round hand as the entries above and below it. It was a perfectly ordinary entry. It said that a ship which had been lying on the rocks past the Teeth for a month, with its master drowned and its cabin full of seawater, had come up the estuary on the flood a week ago and paid its duty like an honest ship.\n\n\"Something interesting, Miss Orrey?\"\n\nShe did not jump, though she wanted to. Edric Vane had a way of arriving beside you that made you feel you had been caught at something even when you had not. He was a tall, soft-spoken man in his fifties, with grey hair worn long over his collar and gold spectacles he took off whenever he wanted to look at you properly. He took them off now.\n\n\"The *Gannet*, sir,\" she said. \"Out of Breme. She's entered inbound eight days ago.\"\n\n\"Is she.\" He did not look at the register. He looked at her.\n\n\"She sank, sir. A month ago. Out past the Teeth. Her master drowned.\"\n\n\"Who told you that?\"\n\nShe hesitated. \"It's known on the Stair, sir. The lightermen know.\"\n\n\"The lightermen.\" Vane smiled. He had a good smile, kind and a little tired, the smile of a man who had explained the same thing to a great many junior clerks. \"Miss Orrey, do you know what a lighterman is paid for a wreck?\"\n\n\"A bounty, sir. A tenth of the salvage.\"\n\n\"A tenth of the salvage, if the wreck is declared. So a lighterman has every reason in the world to find wrecks where there are none, and to tell clerks about them, and to hope that clerks will tell the Exchequer.\" He put his spectacles back on and finally glanced at the page. \"The *Gannet* came in on the twenty-fifth. I remember her. I stamped her myself. Bessaly was in a foul temper about the fog.\"\n\n\"Sir, with respect, I—\"\n\n\"Miss Orrey.\" Gently. \"The register is the register. It is not a newsletter. It does not record what the lightermen say on the Stair. It records what came across the bar and what was paid. If the *Gannet* went down after the twenty-fifth, then I am very sorry for Captain Bessaly, and no doubt the Guild will file for the rebate in due course, and we'll see it then.\" He closed the register, quite softly, with one hand. \"You have your count to finish.\"\n\n\"I've finished it, sir.\"\n\n\"Then you have the Sorrel assessment to deliver this afternoon. Mother Agathe expects it by four. Don't keep her waiting; she's not a patient woman, and she's not a woman I like to have impatient with this office.\" He patted her shoulder once, lightly, and went back through the glass door into his warm room.\n\nTamsin went back to her desk. She sat for a long time with her pen in her hand and did not write anything. The packet against her ribs had warmed to the heat of her body, and she could feel the round lump of the seal through her waistcoat whenever she breathed.\n\nHe had not asked who the lighterman was. She noticed that later, on the stairs, with the Sorrel assessment in its folder under her arm. He had asked who told her, and she had said the Stair, and he had let it go. A careful man, a man who stamped every page himself and remembered the weather on the day, and he had not asked which lighterman.\n\nPerhaps because he did not need to.","wordCount":1050,"createdAt":1794286690203}],"tombstones":[],"blobs":[]}